\par\par \b \uc2 Japan MOF Hayashi: To Act Against Rapid  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Moves   \b0\par\par\uc2 327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2004\par 09:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements become too excessive, as they have been recently, the Finance Ministry's top bureaucrat said Thursday, underscoring Tokyo's resolve to curb yen strength.   \par \par \uc2 "With regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's as we've always said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect economic fundamentals and move stably," Administrative Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 "We believe that recent movements have been rather rapid. When there are rapid moves like this, we will take timely action," Hayashi said.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders say the Finance Ministry, acting through the Bank of Japan, bought between $5 billion and $10 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Wednesday to boost the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The government been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a furious pace this month to keep the dollar supported and the yen subdued, fearing a surging yen will harm exporters and their ability to support the economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 Hayashi said that while the government is not targeting any specific \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, it remains committed to acting against excessive movements.   \par \par \uc2 While some have started to speculate \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may tone down its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in the U.S. next month, Hayashi's comments suggest Tokyo hasn't changed its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The bureaucrat declined to comment directly on Wednesday's decision by the U.S. Federal Reserve's to keep rates steady but adopt a more hawkish-sounding outlook.   \par \par \uc2 But he said he believes the U.S. economy remains firm, and that U.S. policymakers were likely working to sustain that strength.   \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Federal Open Market Committee surprised markets by dropping the phrase that monetary policy would remain accommodative for a "considerable period" of time, replacing it with a pledge it would be "patient" before tightening.   \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040129e01t000bo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
