\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan 28  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 10:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: In yet another reversal, Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra Wednesday acknowledged that a pan-Asian bird-flu outbreak is posing a serious threat to public health and the region's economies. Days earlier, Thaksin had described the emergence of the disease in Thailand as "no big deal," and said the government could easily handle it. Now warning that the bird-flu virus was "no less deadly than SARS," Thaksin called for "quick, decisive action" to contain the spread of the disease in the region and avert a global pandemic among humans. The government is battling a bird-flu outbreak that has now spread to about one-third of the country's provinces, including parts of Bangkok, and has killed at least two people. ("Thai PM/Bird Flu -2: At International Experts Conference" first take ran at 0717 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's inflation rate fell to a four-year low in 2003, mostly reflecting the Australian dollar's sharp rise over the year, and muddying the case for interest rates to be raised another notch. The consumer price index rose 2.4% in the fourth quarter of a 2003 from a year earlier, its meekest annual increase since 1999. ("=WRAP: Australian CPI At 4-Yr Low; Rate Hike Case Unclear" ran at 0358 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: Australia's consumer inflation rate is already low and likely to fall further in coming quarters, but a more medium-term view isn't as benign as the current CPI figures suggest. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: CPI Outlook Not As Benign As It Looks" ran at 0344 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: For once, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are taking the Japanese Finance Ministry at its word. Ministry officials were taken aback at the market's reaction Tuesday when Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki mouthed the official line - that yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aren't designed to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at specific levels - in a routine question-and-answer session in Parliament. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Fears Of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Waning Ahead Of G7" ran at 0813 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =BOJ WATCH: Despite easing monetary policy last week, Bank of Japan board members will likely start quietly discussing how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will exit its ultra-easy policy once deflation is defeated. ("=BOJ Watch: Board May Secretly Debate Exiting Easy Policy" ran at 0537 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's government will continue to focus on rejuvenating the economy this year via expansionary policies amid lingering uncertainties stemming from weak corporate investment and consumer confidence. ("S Korea To Focus On Expansionary Policies To Revive Econ" ran at 0126 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: In its second verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy warned Wednesday that it will take "necessary" action to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. ("S Korea Govt/\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  -2: 2nd Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  This Month" ran at 0016 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: There is no doubt that the Bank of Korea has become more independent of the government since the Asian financial crisis forced a break in the top-down relationship. But the recent resignation of a member of the interest rate-setting monetary policy board in favor of a ministerial position has had the unplanned effect of drawing attention to the distance the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had to travel to walk on its own. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Korea BOK Autonomy Debate Resurfaces" ran at 0559 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese retail sales fell 0.1% on year in December, the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry said Wednesday, suggesting consumers haven't loosened their pursestrings yet despite signs of a recovery in Japan's economy. ("Japan December Retail Sales -2:2nd Straight Month Of Fall" ran at 0007 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: BOS International Australia, a unit of Bank of Scotland Group, hopes to issue about A$200 million to A$300 million in bonds after conducting a debt roadshow in coming weeks. ("=INTERVIEW: Bank of Scotland Hopes For A$ Bond Issue" ran at 0024 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's largest power generator, Meridian Energy, is on the verge of tapping the U.S. private placement market, which has served other New Zealand energy companies well in recent years. ("=NZ Meridian Set To Test Pricing Of US Private Placement" ran at 0256 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar rallied against the yen in Asia Wednesday on renewed caution about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Japanese monetary authorities after the U.S unit had plunged overnight to a new three-and-a-half-year low. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Rallies Vs Yen On Tanigaki Warning" ran at 0548 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Travel- and tourism-related stocks were sold across Asia Wednesday because of the spreading outbreak of bird flu, even though many analysts said investors might be overreacting. The selling spread to hotel, retail and even some consumer goods stocks around the region as investors worried that tourist arrivals and spending might slip. ("=WRAP: Bird Flu Spurs Travel Share Sell-Off Across Asia" ran at 0742 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sony Corp. Wednesday reported an on-year decline in profits for the October-December quarter due to heavy restructuring costs, although sales grew to a record quarterly level thanks to aggressive launches of competitive digital products. The Japanese consumer electronics and entertainment giant also lifted its full-year group net profit outlook, while at the same time predicting heavier restructuring costs as it presses ahead with efforts to put its business back on track. ("Sony -2: Operating Profit At Upper End Of Analysts' Views" ran at 0642 GMT; WRAP will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: National Australia Bank neutralized the impact of its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  scandal after raising nearly A$1 billion from the sale of its strategic holdings in local and U.K. finance-sector rivals. Investors welcomed the back-to-basics approach of Australia's biggest bank, which has shelved plans to undertake costly acquisitions to grow its funds management and overseas banking \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=WRAP: Australia's NAB Neutralizes \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader Losses" ran at 0723 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Military Bank and DBS Thai Danu Bank Wednesday signed a memorandum of understanding to combine their assets in a strategic merger that will create Thailand's sixth-largest banking group in terms of assets. The two parties also agreed to explore incorporating state-run Industrial Finance Copr. of Thailand in a three-way merger to create Thailand's fifth-largest bank. ("Thai Military Bank, DBS Thai Danu Sign Merger MOU" ran at 0748 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and business at fast-food franchise KFC in China are "doing well" and expansion plans haven't been derailed by the latest health crisis, an official at parent firm Yum! Brands Inc. insisted Wednesday, despite the spread of bird flu across Asia. (story will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Dutch company Heineken,, the world's third- largest brewer, said Wednesday its associate Heineken Asia-Pacific Breweries China is buying a 21% stake in Guangdong Brewery for EUR57 million. Heineken will pay HK$1.85 a share to acquire 133.768 million new shares and 165.5 million old shares in the Chinese brewer. ("Guangdong Brewery/Heineken -2: To Pay HK$1.85/Share" ran at 0852 GMT; WRAP will run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Victor Co. of Japan says it plans to shed 990 staff at the parent company, leaving 7,000 workers by March 2007, and reduce the number of its manufacturing sites in Japan and overseas as part of structural reforms to ride out global competition. ("Victor-2: To Cut Global Production Sites To 23-24 From 28" ran at 0241 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Mitsubishi Electric says it will spend about Y10 billion to build five new factories in Japan and abroad to boost its global production of automotive parts. ("Mitsubishi Elec To Spend Y10B To Boost Auto Parts Output" ran at 0309 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Industrial Revitalization Corp., the government-backed body in charge of turning around heavily indebted but viable firms, says it has decided to support ailing water and gas meter maker Kimmon Manufacturing Co. ("Japan IRC To Aid Revival Of Water, Gas Meter Maker Kimmon" first take ran at 0223 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: With South Korean banks in the mood to diversify into other businesses, some are looking into the possibility of taking over LG Investment & Securities, said a senior official at the Financial Supervisory Commission. (INTERVIEW will run around 1015 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Siam Cement, Thailand's largest industrial conglomerate, said Wednesday its 2003 net profit rose 37% to THB19.95 billion, fueled by the strong performance of its core cement and petrochemical businesses. ("Siam Cement/ Earnings -3:Additional Dividend Of THB3.50/Shr" ran at 0723 GMT; see also "Siam Cement/Deb -2: Mulling More Debenture Issues" that ran at 0804 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Daewoo Heavy Industries & Machinery said its 2003 net profit rose 62% to KRW163.4 billion from previous year's KRW100.9 billion.("Korea Daewoo Heavy '03 Net KRW163.4B Vs KRW100.9B" and "S Korea Daewoo Heavy/Earnings -2: Repays KRW148.6B Debt" ran at 0152 GMT and 0210 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Samsung Electro-Mechanics said Wednesday it swung to a fourth-quarter net loss of KRW50.6 billion, mainly due to losses stemming from its credit card affiliate. ("Samsung Electro-Mechanics/Results -3: Loss From Card Co" ran at 0250 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Keppel Land, the property unit of government-linked Keppel group, will release full-year earnings later today. (results expected around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australia's Alumina Ltd. Wednesday said its 2003 net profit rose 13% to A$236.9 million as a China-driven surge in demand helped overcome the impact of a sharp rise in the Australian dollar. ("=WRAP: Australia's Alumina Profit Reveals A$,China Impact" ran at 0553 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Cosmo Oil, Shikoku Electric Power and Shikoku Gas have agreed to set up a joint-venture company to build a liquefied natural gas import tank terminal in Sakaide, Kagawa Prefecture, in western Japan. ("("Cosmo/LNG Venture -2: Tank To Be Completed By 2010" ran at 0500 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040128e01s000d9\par }\p