\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  traders size up Florida G7 after Dubai surprise.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 17:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 28 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers are wrestling with the tricky task of trading the dollar in the uncertain lead-up to next week's Group of Seven finance ministers meeting and its immediate aftermath.  \par \par \uc2 With just over a week to go before finance chiefs and central bankers from the world's seven most developed economies meet on Feb. 6-7 in Boca Raton, Florida, some traders are bracing for a scenario in which the greenback could fare quite differently against the euro than it does against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is likely to continue to depreciate across the board ... although you are likely to see a much stronger yen," by comparison with the dollar's other major counterparts before and after the G7 meeting, said Paresh Upadhyaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with Putnam Investments in Boston.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect a steady stream of protests from European policy-makers about the euro's strength ahead of the meeting, which could give the dollar a slight reprieve against the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 However, traders if anything are growing bolder about pushing the dollar lower against the yen as the gathering of top finance officials from the United States, Canada, Germany, France, the U.K., Japan and Italy approaches.  \par \par \uc2 This is potentially a dangerous game since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can resort to hefty yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that can send the dollar shooting higher, but many U.S.-based analysts reckon the Bank of Japan, which acts on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may avoid the market as the meeting draws near.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen will be an interesting story as we head into G7: traders may start to ponder whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will threaten (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) but not back it up," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston, adding that this could weaken the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders expect the dollar to sag to the 100 yen area in a post-G7 retreat, or perhaps even before.  \par \par \uc2 "We have seen the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  back away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prior to major meetings and that may be something the market is focusing on," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 As soon as a day after the meeting, the dollar could face enough pressure to slip toward the 100 yen zone, an area likely to rouse a vigorous defense from Japan, said Craig Russell, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with online trading platform Alaron \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 YEN POISED TO GAIN  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect finance ministers in Florida to stick with abroad reiteration of the Dubai G7 communique's language on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts would likely take that as a second official nudge to Japan and China to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more room to appreciate against the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 From 117.29 yen on Sept. 12 - about a week before the Dubai meeting - the dollar fell to 114.06 yen on Sept. 19, the day before the G7 Dubai communique which admonished major economies to pursue \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar then slipped to 111.66 yen on Sept. 26 - a decline of 4.8 percent over a two-week period.  \par \par \uc2 This time around, the dollar could be poised to lose about 5 percent against the yen, from its 105.70 yen level early on Wednesday in New York, some analysts expect.  \par \par \uc2 Because now traders are becoming used to the idea - which surprised markets at Dubai - of the G7 implicitly pressuring Japan and China to loosen the reins on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "the big risk is that it (a dollar fall against the yen) happens before (the Florida) G7," said Upadhyaya.  \par \par \uc2 But others expect the dollar will suffer a less steep decline than after the Dubai meeting and will instead resume a slow and steady depreciation against the yen, the euro and other major counterparts.  \par \par \uc2 The upcoming G7 statement is less likely to catch the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market napping than Dubai's surprise departure from traditional language, said Daniel Katzive, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS in Stamford, Connecticut.  \par \par \uc2 This means the dollar will more probably grind lower than slide precipitously, he said, predicting that the euro will rise to fresh record highs of $1.30 over a three-month horizon, up from $1.2560 early on Wednesday in New York. (Additional reporting by Manuela Badawy in New York and Kyle Peterson in Chicago).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040128e01s002wr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
