\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar struggles post BOJ support, FOMC key focus.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 28 (Reuters) - The dollar struggled below 106 yen on Wednesday, near the previous day's three-year low, as suspected Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier failed to deter mounting selling pressure on the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 The early focus was on the battle between dollar bears and the Bank of Japan but traders were also awaiting the outcome of the U.S. Federal Reserve's monetary policy meeting later for clues as to how long U.S. interest rates would remain low.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback sold off across the board on Tuesday after a disappointing U.S. consumer confidence report. But in Tokyo trade on Wednesday it jumped nearly one yen to 106.62, making dealers suspect authorities had taken action, although no confirmation was available.  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the international bureau at the Finance Ministry, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's past \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not excessive and was needed. He also said the Group of Seven nations will seek to avoid misguiding the market about their policy when they issue a statement at their February meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  pretty tactically. It also made clear \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want anything from G7 that would let the yen appreciate," said Mary Davis, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CSFB.  \par \par \uc2 "The Fed decision will be no change and the statement will be similar to last time. It will put the dollar under downward pressure as it will confirm rates will stay low for some time."  \par \par \uc2 By 1212 GMT the dollar was holding at 105.78 yen, just above Tuesday's three-year low around 105.45 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Against the euro it was up a quarter of a percent on the day at $1.2606, having shed around 1-1/2 cents on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was firmer against the dollar and the euro after Prime Minister Tony Blair narrowly avoided defeat in a parliamentary vote late on Tuesday and a newspaper said on Wednesday he had been exonerated in an inquiry into the suicide of a UK weapons expert last year.  \par \par \uc2 FED ON HOLD  \par \par \uc2 The Fed's Open Market Committee is due to announce its rate decision at 1915 GMT. No change is expected in U.S. rates, currently at a 45-year low of 1.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Instead the focus is on what language the Fed will use to characterise the state of the economy. Analysts expect it will say the economy is improving solidly, inflation is low and labour markets improving only modestly, meaning rates will be on hold for a "considerable period", as in its previous assessment.  \par \par \uc2 "There may be some risk (for euro bulls) going into the FOMC tonight, especially if the Fed sounds more positive on the economy," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. data later includes durable goods and new homes sales.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN/EUROZONE RHETORIC  \par \par \uc2 The yen was boosted in recent days by talk that the February G7 meeting would result in pressure on Asia to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise to help correct global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "Everyone was expecting them to abstain from the market and the fact that they've come straight back in really makes the forthcoming G7 meeting particularly interesting as the euro zone contingent is likely to have something to say about it," said Neil Mellor, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Japan's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said recent moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were slightly rapid and that Japanese authorities would act against any overshooting.  \par \par \uc2 From the euro zone, officials kept hinting that volatility was unwelcome but cutting rates was not an option to curb euro strength. German economy minister Wolfgang Clement said there was no need at present for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates or engage in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040128e01s0024v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
