\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan says G7 wants to avoid misguiding markets.  \b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 11:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 28 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven industrial nations will seek to avoid giving misleading impressions to the markets about their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy when they issue a statement at their meeting next week, a Japanese official said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, the head of the international bureau at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also defended \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hefty \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year, saying the authorities stepped in out of necessity.  \par \par \uc2 The remarks come a day after the dollar plunged to three-year lows around 105.50 yen The dollar's broad-based decline, which took the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to life lows against the euro earlier this month, accelerated after a G7 communique last September called for greater exchange rate flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 "We must look at it from the standpoint of preventing misleading information going out to the market," Watanabe told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 "There is an existing communique, so any new wording will be based on that," he added, responding to questions about whether Japan would ask its G7 counterparts to drop the word "flexibility" from a new communique at the February 6-7 meeting in Florida.  \par \par \uc2 Growing concern expressed by European officials this month about the euro's strength has fuelled anticipation of a change in the wording in the communique.  \par \par \uc2 But at the same time Europe is seen to be frustrated by the relative inflexibility of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which some feel could be forcing the euro to shoulder an undue amount of the burden of correcting the bloated U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Asked what \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could tell its G7 partners about its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which ballooned to a record 20 trillion yen ($189.4 billion) last year, Watanabe referred to Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki's repeated remarks that Tokyo will take appropriate action as necessary.  \par \par \uc2 "As the minister has said, we do it because it is necessary. It has not been excessive," he said.  \par \par \uc2 On speculation in the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could scale down \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just ahead of the G7 - a view sparked by Tanigaki's remarks on Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at smoothing volatility, not at keeping the yen at a certain level - Watanabe said it was a result of malicious misinterpretation.  \par \par \uc2 "Some people intentionally quote just a part of the whole statement," Watanabe said.  \par \par \uc2 "The minister was merely stating that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not be aimed at moving the exchange rate to a certain level and keeping it there. He also said we will take decisive action against moves that do not reflect economic fundamentals." ($1=105.59 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040128e01s001ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
