\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-BOJ seen  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  to stem yen's renewed rise.  \b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 08:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 28 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan (BOJ) was suspected of buying dollars on Wednesday to stem the yen's renewed strength a day after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a fresh three-year high, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar suddenly jumped in late Asian trade after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying the dollar at about 105.80 yen and appeared to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to prevent the yen's resurgence, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  The greenback surged to a high of 106.65 yen shortly after the suspected BOJ action before falling back to 105.76/82 as of 0748 GMT, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  A source at the Finance Ministry, which gives instructions for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the BOJ, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remained unchanged, but declined to comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  Non-Japanese fund operators were detected dumping the dollar after it shot up to the day's high.   \par \par \uc2  A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in Tokyo said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through several Japanese banks.   \par \par \uc2  "One major Japanese bank was detected bidding heavily and other Japanese banks followed. Those heavy bids are most likely backed by the BOJ's orders," the trader said.   \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's action comes as the dollar faces fresh downward pressure amid increasing doubts about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The dollar slumped to fresh three-year lows around 105.45 yen  on Tuesday after Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was not aimed at keeping the yen at a certain level.   \par \par \uc2  Although Tanigaki said on Wednesday that recent moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were slightly rapid and that Japan would act against any overshooting, the dollar stayed vulnerable to strong speculative selling.   \par \par \uc2  The ministry source added that Tanigaki's remarks on Tuesday on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not represent a policy change.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said the BOJ was trying to defend the dollar from falling sharply below 106 yen again.   \par \par \uc2  "I think they were trying to show that their stance has not changed, especially after Tanigaki's comments yesterday," said a trader at a Japanese bank. "They are likely to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trying to keep the dollar above 106 yen."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040128e01s0017a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
