\par\par \b \uc2 Tokyo Shares End Lower; Tech Stocks Fall Ahead Of Sony 3Q  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazuhiro Shimamura  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 918 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2004\par 07:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks ended slightly lower Wednesday as Wall Street's retreat and the stronger yen hurt technology stocks, while many players withdrew to the sidelines ahead of earnings data from Sony.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei 225 Stock Average ended down 75.56 points, or 0.7%, to 10852.47, falling for the third consecutive session. The index lost 44.57 points Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, after an initial fall the selling momentum faded, leaving the Nikkei 225 above technical support around 10800 points.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was affected by U.S. shares overnight as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, but these aren't entirely fresh concerns that would push the market beyond the lower end of its recent trading range," said Shoji Yoshigoe, an investment research manager at UFJ Tsubasa Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also said investors were reluctant to build positions before the results of the U.S. Federal Reserve Open Market Committee meeting due out later in the global day, though most expect the Fed to leave policy settings unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 Sony ended the day off 0.2% at Y4,320. After the close, Sony raised its group net profit forecast for the year through March to Y55 billion from Y50 billion, though October-December sales in its electronics division rose only 0.4% despite aggressive launches of new products.  \par \par \uc2 Among other technology blue chips, Toshiba fell 1.8% to Y447, NEC shed 2.2% to Y836, and Fujitsu sagged 2.8% to Y688.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the Nikkei may remain top-heavy this week, though many expect to see improvements in technology companies earnings in their October-December results, due out over the coming sessions.  \par \par \uc2 The Topix index of all Tokyo Stock Exchange First Section issues declined 5.21 points, or 0.5%, to 1058.15.  \par \par \uc2 Volume on the TSE First Section came to 1.120 billion shares, slightly up from 1.061 billion shares Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 On the First Section, 997 issues fell, 409 issues rose, and 125 ended unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 UFJ Tsubasa's Yoshigoe said the Nikkei 225 may have already factored in leading technology firms' expected quarterly results and forecasts for the year through March.  \par \par \uc2 If so, "selling on fact" could keep the Nikkei top-heavy for a week or two unless surprisingly strong figures come out in the earnings announcements later this week and early next week, Yoshigoe said.  \par \par \uc2 The selling in microchip testing equipment maker Advantest was a good indication of the possibly cool market reaction to upcoming earnings news, he added. Advantest ended down 5.2% at Y8,740 after posting solid third quarter results Tuesday and raising its forecast for the year through March.  \par \par \uc2 NEC Electronics, which also reported strong results Tuesday, finished down 4.2% at Y7,500.  \par \par \uc2 NEC, Fujitsu and Toshiba will report their results this week, followed by Matsushita Electric Industrial, Sharp and Hitachi early next week.  \par \par \uc2 Advantest's shares were affected by overnight declines in U.S. counterparts, but the same thing could happen to other technology companies, said a local brokerage manager.  \par \par \uc2 "Stocks like Sony have been attracting a lot of attention lately, but looking at the recent trend of U.S. shares, this is time to be prudent," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Sony's results contained mixed signals, but Japanese authorities' apparent efforts to limit the yen's rise may buoy sentiment on the technology sector, where many firms rely on exports for much of their earnings.  \par \par \uc2 Less than an hour after the market was closed, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  jumped by almost one yen against the dollar to around Y106.60 in what traders suspect was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the behest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Among exporter automakers, Nissan slipped 1.2% to Y1,159 and Honda shed 1.1% to Y4,430. Toyota ended up 1.1% at Y3,590.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, fishery stocks were the best performing sector by far, bought on hopes for stronger consumer demand for seafood amid spreading bird flu fears.  \par \par \uc2 Maruha jumped 12.5% to Y171, Kyokuyo climbed 7.2% to Y194, and Nippon Suisan added 3.2% to Y259.  \par \par \uc2 Water and gas meter maker Kimmon Manufacturing rocketed 25.4% to Y143 on the state-backed Industrial Revitalization Corp.'s decision to support the company.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the weakness in blue chips led retail investors to go after low-priced issues and newly listed stocks in the hope of quick profit-taking later.  \par \par \uc2 The first initial public offerings for this year enjoyed remarkable gains Wednesday. Market research firm Macromill debuted on the TSE's Mother's market at Y1.51 million, well exceeding its IPO price of Y700,000. It finished at Y1.68 million.  \par \par \uc2 BrainNavi, a book publishing service company, debuted on the Osaka bourse's Hercules market but didn't trade due to a rush of buy orders. It ended bid-only at Y238,000, way above its IPO price of Y103,000.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                            Close    Previous   Change \par                                        Close \par Nikkei 225 Stock Avg    10852.47    10928.03   -75.56 \par Nikkei 300                206.42      207.18    -0.76 \par Topix Index              1058.15     1063.36    -5.21 \par First Section Volume  