\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar drops on soft U.S. data, Japan remarks.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2004\par 22:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 27 (Reuters) - The dollar dropped more than 1 percent against the euro and Swiss franc and hit a three-year low against the yen on Tuesday as signs of labor market softness in a U.S. consumer confidence report surprised traders.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback was also pressured by comments from one Japanese policy-maker who said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at smoothing volatility, not at keeping the yen at a specific level.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell below a three-year low of 105.70 yen set earlier in January, hitting fresh three-year lows on Tuesday around 105.47 yen according to Reuters data. The dollar was pressured by speculation that Japan would limit its sales of yen before the Feb. 6-7 meeting of Group of Seven industrial nations finance ministers in Florida.  \par \par \uc2 Late on Tuesday afternoon in New York, the dollar traded at 105.62 yen, down 0.6 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The yen firmed against the dollar after Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was aimed at smoothing volatility and not at keeping the yen at a certain level.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at removing volatility from the market," Tanigaki said in response to parliamentary questions. "We don't use it to keep the yen at a certain level or lead the yen lower."  \par \par \uc2 "The Japanese finance minister's comments overnight allowed the market to push the dollar lower," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "We have seen the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  back away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prior to major meetings and that may be something the market is focusing on," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 TOUGH U.S. JOB SEARCH  \par \par \uc2 U.S. consumer confidence improved in January but Americans were still having a tough time finding jobs, the Conference Board said. Its index of confidence jumped to 96.8 in January from a revised 91.7 in December.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts had forecast a rise to 96.3, but traders said the market expected an even better report and as a result sold the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "It is consistent with a U.S. economy continuing to gain ground in the first half of the year. But at the margin, it is disappointing relative to expectations," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro  traded up 1.4 percent on the day to $1.2639 according to\par Reuters data, while the Australian dollar was up 1.2 percent versus the\par greenback to US$0.7787       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell about 1.3 percent against the Swiss franc to trade around 1.2400 francs .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market had bought quite a few dollars back in the last few days and it didn't find a solid reason to continue that process," said Sinche of Citibank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pound was trading at $1.8273, up 0.8 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling rose briefly against the dollar after British Prime Minister Tony Blair avoided his first major parliamentary defeat when lawmakers voted narrowly to approve his controversial education bill.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger-than-expected German business confidence index helped the euro rise against the dollar in European trade.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The influential German Ifo economic research institute, which polls about 7,000 firms each month, said its index rose to 97.4 in January, the highest since J"Committee's two-day policy meeting started on Tuesday. With no interest rate change expected, the market is waiting to see whether the Fed's statement drops a reference to rates remaining on hold for a "considerable period" when the FOMC makes its decision on Wednesday. (Additional reporting by John Parry and Daniel Bases in New York).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040127e01r006c1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
