\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rallies as European talk curbs euro strength.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry   \par \uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2004\par 22:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 26 (Reuters) - The dollar rallied against several major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday on talk by European officials about curbing euro strength ahead of next week's meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrial nations.   \par \par \uc2 Traders' expectations that the G7 may nudge Japan and China to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  more room to appreciate against the dollar lifted the yen, especially against the euro, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 The euro's drop of more than 1 percent against the yen contributed to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall versus the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "The break of the euro (below) 133 yen has put some pressure on euro/dollar," said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan in New York.   \par \par \uc2 "There is a growing sense in the market that the G7 meeting is going to have some sort of language as it did in September (at the G7 meeting in Dubai) suggesting that Asians should take part in the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebalancing (that is) underway ... that is convincing investors to buy yen," Patterson said.   \par \par \uc2 The euro slid 1.2 percent against the yen to 132.52 yen , and 0.91 percent against the dollar to $1.2471 in late New York trading.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed 1.2 percent against the Swiss franc to 1.2570 francs.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 0.3 percent to 106.29 yen , while the dollar sagged to three-year lows against the yen not far below the 106.0 yen level hit earlier this month.   \par \par \uc2 Sterling slipped 0.8 percent to $1.8114.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the global day, European politicians expressed mixed opinions on the need for action to curb volatile exchange rates.   \par \par \uc2 "One of the reasons for the choppiness today is because of a fairly steady stream of comments coming out of the euro zone on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Nick Bennenbroek, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Brown Brothers Harriman & Co. in New York.   \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  contradicted French Finance Minister Francis Mer's weekend comment that ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet discussed interest rates in a meeting with euro zone finance ministers last week, sending the euro briefly higher.   \par \par \uc2 G7 finance officials gathered in Brussels on Monday as part of a wider meeting of G10 officials and academics.   \par \par \uc2 The Irish Finance Minister Charlie McCreevy said in a statement for delivery before a European Parliament committee: "It was made clear by Eurogroup ministers and the ECB that our long-term strategy does not change: the euro must keep its value over the medium-and long run, in line with economic fundamentals."   \par \par \uc2 McCreevy, as well as Dutch Finance Minister Gerrit Zalm, stressed stability and showed concern over excessive exchange rate moves.   \par \par \uc2 From the U.S. side of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, U.S. Undersecretary for Trade Grant Aldonas said in Paris: "The important thing is not to focus so much on exchange rates but on the underlying economic fundamentals."   \par \par \uc2 "The market is focusing more on these (politicians') comments than they would do otherwise simply because there is nothing going on and because the markets are a little thin," Bennenbroek said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar weakened against the yen earlier on Monday after the Japanese Finance Ministry reported that its trade surplus expanded for the fifth time in six months in December and on commercial selling and exporters' demand for yen.   \par \par \uc2 While Japan's trade surplus is one factor buoying the yen, the wide U.S. trade deficit remains a heavy weight on the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The head of Japan's largest business lobby said on Monday that he hoped the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the dollar falls below 105 yen because most companies have said their assumed dollar/yen rate is 105 to 110 yen.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar mostly shrugged off a stronger-than-expected rise in December sales of existing U.S. homes. Sales rose 6.9 percent, according to the National Association of Realtors.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is also keenly awaiting a two-day meeting of the Federal Reserve's Federal Open Market Committee meeting, starting on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Although the Fed is not expected to change interest rates, traders will scrutinize the wording of the statement it issues after the meeting on Wednesday for clues on whether the Fed may raise rates later in the year.   \par \par \uc2 (Manuela Badawy in New York and Justyna Pawlak contributed to this report).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040127e01q0016o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
