\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 5-G7 want rise in Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , no dollar drop-economist.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Carrel  \par \uc2 869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2004\par 19:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BRUSSELS, Jan 26 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven leading industrial nations want to put pressure on China and other Asian nations to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, said Fred Bergsten who attended a meeting with G7 deputy finance ministers on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Bergsten, director of the Institute for International Economics in Washington, also told Reuters the G7 wanted a gradual appreciation of Chinese and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid a sudden free fall in the value of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "They (the G7) want to put pressure on the Asians, particularly China, to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise in value and they want to do all this without moving too fast so that would risk a free fall in the dollar," Bergsten said after attending a meeting in Brussels which included German Deputy Finance Minister Caio Koch-Weser.  \par \par \uc2 Deputy finance ministers and other top officials of the G7 countries - the United States, Japan, Germany, Britain, France, Italy, and Canada - met in Brussels on Sunday evening and Monday morning to prepare for meeting of G7 finance ministers in Florida on February 6-7, according to G7 sources.  \par \par \uc2 Bergsten's comments echo speculation in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that the big economic powers want to smooth the depreciation of the dollar by allowing the yen and Chinese yuan to rise.  \par \par \uc2 At their last meeting in Dubai in September, the G7 called for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in remarks that markets saw as aimed at urging Asian economic powers of China and South Korea to stop fixing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so tightly to the falling U.S dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But with these nations showing few signs of changing their policy, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  still committed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow any rise in the yen, the dollar has mainly declined against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has shot up 11 percent against the dollar in the past four months, hitting record highs, and setting off alarm bells among euro zone politicians who fear the exports they rely on to lead economic recovery may be badly hit.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet was quoted as restating the euro zone's worry over sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't like either excessive volatility nor excessive variations of exchange rates," he said in an interview with Spanish newspaper ABC when asked about the impact of the strong euro on the European economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Bergsten said the G7 faced a delicate task in engineering a gradual rise in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  without a sharp drop in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "They (the G7) can't just say it so clearly as I just did, they have to use code words and proxies, so I think that's a tough task."  \par \par \uc2 "I think they are working towards that. I think there is good prospect for achieving it, but I think it is not yet clear," he added.  \par \par \uc2 But U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said the meeting in Florida which he will chair would have speeding up global growth at the top of its agenda.  \par \par \uc2 "The need for increased global economic growth, by the way, will be the top agenda item," Snow said in remarks for delivery at a business conference in London.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, U.S. Undersecretary for Trade Grant Aldonas told a seminar in Paris, "The important thing is not to focus so much on exchange rates but on the underlying economic fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 NO INTEREST RATE DISCUSSION - TRICHET  \par \par \uc2 However, with speculation swirling that if the euro rises further the ECB could contemplate cutting interest rates, the bank denied that Trichet had discussed rates with euro zone finance ministers last week.  \par \par \uc2 French minister Francis Mer told a news conference at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland, on Saturday that such a discussion took place.  \par \par \uc2 But Mer's office had no comment after an ECB spokesman said on Trichet's behalf: "The minister of finance of France has been misunderstood. He (Trichet) would like you to know there was no discussion on interest rates (at the January 19 meeting)."  \par \par \uc2 Spanish Finance Minister Rodrigo Rato said Trichet had told the London conference at which Snow also spoke he favoured a strong euro, but was worried by sudden movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  thinks that a stable and strong euro is good for Europe, good for Europe's future," Rato quoted Trichet as saying.  \par \par \uc2 This view was endorsed by Dutch Finance Minister Gerrit Zalm who told reporters: "Our main concern is especially rapid movements in the euro." Zalm declined comment on whether the ECB should cut interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The euro zone's key interest rate stands at 2.00 percent, compared with 1.00 percent in the United States. Politicians cannot dictate monetary policy to the independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ECB's Governing Council is next due to discuss interest rates on February 5, the day before the G7 meeting begins.  \par \par \uc2 The weak dollar has also alarmed Japanese officials due to its impact on exporters, whose recent strength is helping lead the economy out of a decades-long slump.  \par \par \uc2 The head of Japan's business lobby said he hoped the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar fell below 105/yen.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040126e01q0030d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
