\par\par \b \uc2 SNAPSHOT-G7 positions on dollar ahead of Florida meeting.   \b0\par\par\uc2 919 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2004\par 14:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 26 (Reuters) - Finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven (G7) most developed economies are scheduled to meet in Florida on February 6 and 7 amid concern about the steep fall of the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Following is a snapshot of developments and key facts:   \par \par \uc2 LATEST NEWS   \par \par \uc2 * The euro and the dollar seesawed in volatile trade and the greenback slid towards three year lows on the yen, as Europe and U.S. officials lined up on opposite sides of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy ahead of a key G7 meeting.   \par \par \uc2 * ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet was quoted as speaking out against "excessive changes in exchange rates". But U.S. Undersecretary for Trade Grant Aldonas said the G7 meeting in Florida should concentrate primarily on economic fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2 * The Spanish and Dutch euro zone finance ministers expressed concern about the speed of the euro's rise but declined to comment on whether the ECB should cut interest rates to help offset the surge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 * The current strength of the euro against the dollar is not causing German steelmakers undue problems as most exports are not priced in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Dieter Ameling, the head of the industry' umbrella group said.   \par \par \uc2 * The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said President Jean-Claude Trichet did not discuss interest rates when meeting euro zone finance ministers last week. French Finance Minister Francis Mer said at the World Economic Forum in Davos at the weekend that euro zone ministers discussed an interest rate cut with him.   \par \par \uc2 * Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates required attention as Japan's economy extended its moderate recovery, underscoring Japan's interest in an issue that is likely to be the hottest topic at the G7 meeting.   \par \par \uc2 * Hiroshi Okuda, chairman of the Japan Business Federation (Keidanren) said he hoped the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the dollar fell below 105 yen.   \par \par \uc2 * IMF first deputy managing director Anne Krueger said on Saturday neither \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  nor interest rate cuts were warranted with the euro at current levels against the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be left to markets and policymakers should only consider action "in the most extreme circumstances".   \par \par \uc2 KEY QUOTES OF TODAY   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 "He (ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet) would like you to know there was no discussion on interest rates when he and the Vice-President (Lucas Papademos) attended the Eurogroup meeting (on January 19)," an ECB spokesman said. "Interest rates are discussed in the Governing Council of the ECB and not anywhere else."   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 "The important thing is not to focus so much on exchange rates but on the underlying economic fundamentals (at the G7 meeting)," U.S. Undersecretary for Trade Grant Aldonas said.   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 "We don't like either excessive volatility nor excessive variations of exchange rates," ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet said in an interview with Spanish newspaper ABC.   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 "Our main concern is especially rapid movements in the euro," Dutch Finance Minister Gerrit Zalm told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 "Abrupt (exchange rate) corrections can be damaging..." Spanish Finance Minister Rodrigo Rato told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 "Most companies have said their assumed dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate is between 105 and 110 yen and their euro/yen rate is between 125 and 130 yen. If the market goes beyond these ranges, I expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Hiroshi Okuda, chairman of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Business Federation (Keidanren).   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 "Our economy is recovering moderately and we can expect it to be sustained, but the pace of recovery will be limited by excessive debts and other structural factors," Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said. "Moves in the financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market require attention during this period."   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 IMF first deputy managing director Anne Krueger said that at current euro/dollar rates "there is a strong presumption against" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I know of no evidence as yet that this is the most extreme of circumstances - or even close. I emphasise 'as yet'."   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 KEY UPCOMING EVENTS - see for more detail   \par \par \uc2 MONDAY   \par \par \uc2 * G7 deputy finance ministers meet in Brusssels to work on the agenda for the February meeting in Florida.   \par \par \uc2 * UK Chancellor Brown hosts conference on Monday, Jan 26 with German Finance Minister Eichel and Bank of England Governor King, plus U.S. Treasury's Snow and U.S. Fed chief Greenspan (1715 GMT) via video link.   \par \par \uc2 * Eurogroup chairman and Irish Finance Minister Charlie McCreevy appears before EU Parliament committee (1530 GMT).   \par \par \uc2 * European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet is scheduled to speak in Frankfurt at the year's opening of the Neue Boerse, hosted by the Deutsche Boerse (1800 GMT).   \par \par \uc2 KEY FACTS   \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers - from U.S., Japan, Germany, France, Britain, Italy and Canada - last met in Dubai on Sept 20, 2003. The key passage of their communiqui said   \par \par \uc2 "We reaffirm that exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals. We continue to monitor exchange markets closely and co-operate as appropriate. In this context we emphasise that more flexibility in exchange rates is desirable for major countries or economic areas to promote smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."   \par \par \uc2 For full text of their statement then,   \par \par \uc2 For full text of last week's Eurogroup/ECB statement,   \par \par \uc2 Since the Dubai meeting, the dollar has fallen:   \par \par \uc2 -10.57 percent against the British pound   \par \par \uc2 -11.68 percent against the euro   \par \par \uc2 -7.09 percent against the yen   \par \par \uc2 -2.75 percent against the Canadian dollar   \par \par \uc2 -8.82 percent on a trade-weighted basis   \par \par \uc2 -   \par \par \uc2 For analysis of the G7 situation:.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040126e01q002se\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
