\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - WRAPUP 1-Stocks end week down - falling euro hurts bonds, gold.   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,098 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2004\par 02:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     In the Jan 23. story headlined "WRAPUP 1-Stocks end week\par  down; falling euro hurts bonds, gold," please read in second\par  paragraph "...further strength in the euro next month could\par  lead to a cut in European interest rates" instead of\par  "...further strength in the euro next month could lead to a\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rise in European interest rates." (corrects likely direction of interest rates)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 A corrected repetition follows:   \par \par \uc2 By Gary Hill   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 23 (Reuters) - Blue chips fell on Friday, with both the Dow and the technology-driven Nasdaq marking their first down week in more than a month as investors were cautious before the thick of earnings reporting season.   \par \par \uc2 The euro fell as much as 1 percent against the dollar after a diplomatic source told Reuters further strength in the euro next month could lead to a cut in European interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 That set off a chain reaction, driving down the prices of bonds and gold. Oil ended almost unchanged.   \par \par \uc2 With a storm of earnings reports looming next week, stock investors made the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average and the Nasdaq opposite ends of a see-saw each of the four days of a week shortened by the Martin Luther King holiday.   \par \par \uc2 But both market gauges ended the week down. The Dow slipped 0.3 percent, breaking eight straight weeks of gains. The Nasdaq fell 0.8 percent, breaking a six-week streak. But the broader S&P edged 0.2 percent higher for its ninth straight up week.   \par \par \uc2 In Friday's heavy trading, the Dow .DJI) fell 54.89 points, or 0.52 percent, to 10,568.29 and the Standard & Poor's 500 Index .SPX) eased 2.39 points, or 0.21 percent, to 1,141.55.   \par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite Index .IXIC) rose 4.86 points, or 0.23 percent, to 2,123.87, based on the latest data.   \par \par \uc2 "People are primarily taking profits today," said Cary Nordan, vice president and equity analyst at BB&T Asset Management in Raleigh, North Carolina.   \par \par \uc2 "Overall, the tone has been positive, but investors are looking ahead to the next six to 12 months and concerned there's going to be a deceleration in earnings growth relative to last year."   \par \par \uc2 MICROSOFT GIVES LIFT   \par \par \uc2 Technology stocks pared gains from the morning, although shares of industry bellwether Microsoft Corp. rallied a day after it lifted its revenue forecast.   \par \par \uc2 Monday will kick off one of the busiest weeks in corporate America's quarterly reporting period. Though earnings reported so far have generally been strong, analysts say many companies have not delivered bullish enough forecasts to justify the steep rise in stock prices.   \par \par \uc2 "The good earnings reports don't really create a stampede of buyers," said Paul Cherney, chief market analyst at Standard & Poor's Marketscope.   \par \par \uc2 "The market expected to see good earnings, and here they are. So now what do we do? What's next to lift prices?" Cherney asked.   \par \par \uc2 Microsoft shares topped the Nasdaq's most actively traded list, rising 47 cents, or 1.7 percent, to $28.48, and were among the Dow's top percentage gainers.   \par \par \uc2 The world's largest software maker on Thursday reported a 17 percent drop in quarterly earnings due to a change in its stock compensation plan, but it raised its revenue outlook, predicting stronger personal computer demand.   \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley shares fell after the investment bank cut its previously reported earnings because of a $27 million after-tax charge after a Paris court awarded damages in connection with its research on French luxury goods group LVMH   \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley said it would take the charge in its fiscal 2003 fourth quarter, lowering its previously reported results by 2 cents per share. Its shares fell 57 cents to $59.29.   \par \par \uc2 In a deal that will create one of the largest banks based in the U.S. Southeast, Regions Financial Corp. and Union Planters Corp. agreed to merge, a $5.9 billion deal. Shares of Regions jumped $2.00, or 5.3 percent, to $39.75, while Union Planters' gained $1.31, or 4.2 percent, to $32.67.   \par \par \uc2 EURO DROPS   \par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro fell 1 percent against the dollar after a diplomatic source told Reuters that euro zone ministers attending a Group of Seven meeting next month will say that further strength in the euro could cause the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 After the comments, the euro EUR=)fell to a session low of $1.2567 from Thursday's close of $1.2712. By Friday's New York close, the euro was at $1.2586, still more than 2-1/2 cents higher against the dollar for the week.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.60 percent to 106.63 yen, recovering from a near three-year low of 105.76 yen, and above Thursday's U.S. close at 105.99 yen. The market remains on guard for possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 After the dollar spiked, Treasury prices slid on fears that foreign central banks' appetite for U.S. government debt could ebb.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury note US10YT=RR slid 29/32 to a price of 101-12/32, driving its yield up to 4.08 percent from an early four-month trough of 3.92 percent. The note closed with its yield at 3.96 percent on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Likewise, the 30-year bond US30YT=RR tumbled 1-19/32 to 106-12/32, taking its yield up to 4.95 percent, when earlier it had been celebrating a six-month low of 4.82 percent. On Thursday, the bond's yield was 4.84 percent at the close.   \par \par \uc2 Precious metals ended mixed in New York as gold eased in sympathy with the fall in the euro and palladium retreated from recent sharp gains on profit-taking, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 COMEX February gold fell $2.10 to $408.00 an ounce. NYMEX March palladium O#PL) fell $11.70 to $242.65 an ounce, cutting into Thursday's rise of $14.20 to a nine-month high.   \par \par \uc2 NYMEX March crude oil futures CLH4) ended practically unchanged, settling at $34.94 a barrel, up 1 cent.   \par \par \uc2 Overseas, the FTSE-100 index .FTSE) slipped 16 points or 0.36 percent to 4,460.8. In Tokyo, the benchmark Nikkei average .N225) gained 68.31 points or 0.62 percent to finish at 11,069.01. (Reporting by Vivian Chu, Pedro Nicolaci da Costa, Gene Ramos, Manuela Badawy and Alden Bentley).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040126e01q0008h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
