\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - WRAPUP 1-Stocks end week down - falling euro hurts bonds, gold.   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,030 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2004\par 02:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     In the Jan 23. story headlined "WRAPUP 1-Stocks end week\par  down; falling euro hurts bonds, gold," please read in second\par  paragraph "...further strength in the euro next month could\par  lead to a cut in European interest rates" instead of\par  "...further strength in the euro next month could lead to a\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2  rise in European interest rates." (corrects likely direction of interest rates)    \par \par \uc2  A corrected repetition follows:    \par \par \uc2  By Gary Hill    \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 23 (Reuters) - Blue chips fell on Friday, with both the Dow and the technology-driven Nasdaq marking their first down week in more than a month as investors were cautious before the thick of earnings reporting season.    \par \par \uc2  The euro fell as much as 1 percent against the dollar after a diplomatic source told Reuters further strength in the euro next month could lead to a cut in European interest rates.    \par \par \uc2  That set off a chain reaction, driving down the prices of bonds and gold. Oil ended almost unchanged.    \par \par \uc2  With a storm of earnings reports looming next week, stock investors made the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average and the Nasdaq opposite ends of a see-saw each of the four days of a week shortened by the Martin Luther King holiday.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Friday's heavy trading, the Dow  fell 54.89 points, or 0.52 percent,\par to 10,568.29 and the Standard & Poor's 500 Index        eased 2.39\par points, or 0.21 percent, to 1,141.55.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Nasdaq Composite Index  rose 4.86 points, or 0.23 percent, to\par 2,123.87, based on the latest data.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People are primarily taking profits today," said Cary Nordan, vice\par president and equity analyst at BB&T Asset Management in Raleigh, North\par Carolina.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Overall, the tone has been positive, but investors are looking ahead\par to the next six to 12 months and concerned there's going to be a\par deceleration in earnings growth relative to last year."\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MICROSOFT GIVES LIFT\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Technology stocks pared gains from the morning, although shares of\par industry bellwether Microsoft Corp. (MSFT.O) rallied a day after it\par lifted its revenue forecast.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monday will kick off one of the busiest weeks in corporate America's\par quarterly reporting period. Though earnings reported so far have\par generally been strong, analysts say many companies have not delivered\par bullish enough forecasts to justify the steep rise in stock prices.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The good earnings reports don't really create a stampede of buyers,"\par said Paul Cherney, chief market analyst at Standard & Poor's\par Marketscope.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market expected to see good earnings, and here they are. So now\par what do we do? What's next to lift prices?" Cherney asked.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Microsoft shares topped the Nasdaq's most actively traded list, rising\par 47 cents, or 1.7 percent, to $28.48, and were among the Dow's top\par percentage gainers.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The world's largest software maker on Thursday reported a 17 percent\par drop in quarterly earnings due to a change in its stock compensation\par plan, but it raised its revenue outlook, predicting stronger personal\par computer demand.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Morgan Stanley (MWD.N) shares fell after the investment bank cut its\par previously reported earnings because of a $27 million after-tax charge\par after a Paris court awarded damages in connection with its research on\par French luxury goods group LVMH (LVMH.PA).\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Morgan Stanley said it would take the charge in its fiscal 2003 fourth\par quarter, lowering its previously reported results by 2 cents per share.\par Its shares fell 57 cents to $59.29.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In a deal that will create one of the largest banks based in the U.S.\par Southeast, Regions Financial Corp. (RF.N) and Union Planters Corp.\par (UPC.N) agreed to merge, a $5.9 billion deal. Shares of Regions jumped\par $2.00, or 5.3 percent, to $39.75, while Union Planters' gained $1.31,\par or 4.2 percent, to $32.67.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EURO DROPS\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, the euro fell 1 percent against the dollar\par after a diplomatic source told Reuters that euro zone ministers\par attending a Group of Seven meeting next month will say that further\par strength in the euro could cause the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut\par interest rates.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After the comments, the euro  fell to a session low of $1.2567 from\par Thursday's close of $1.2712. By Friday's New York close, the euro was\par at $1.2586, still more than 2-1/2 cents higher against the dollar for\par the week.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar rose 0.60 percent to 106.63 yen, recovering from a near\par three-year low of 105.76 yen, and above Thursday's U.S. close at 105.99\par yen. The market remains on guard for possible yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After the dollar spiked, Treasury prices slid on fears that foreign\par central banks' appetite for U.S. government debt could ebb.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury note  slid 29/32 to a price of\par 101-12/32, driving its yield up to 4.08 percent from an early\par four-month trough of 3.92 percent. The note closed with its yield at\par 3.96 percent on Thursday.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Likewise, the 30-year bond  tumbled 1-19/32 to 106-12/32, taking its\par yield up to 4.95 percent, when earlier it had been celebrating a\par six-month low of 4.82 percent. On Thursday, the bond's yield was 4.84\par percent at the close.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Precious metals ended mixed in New York as gold eased in sympathy with\par the fall in the euro and palladium retreated from recent sharp gains on\par profit-taking, traders said.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par COMEX February gold fell $2.10 to $408.00 an ounce. NYMEX March\par palladium  fell $11.70 to $242.65 an ounce, cutting into Thursday's\par rise of $14.20 to a nine-month high.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Overseas, the FTSE-100 index  slipped 16 points or 0.36 percent to\par 4,460.8. In Tokyo, the benchmark Nikkei average         gained 68.31\par points or 0.62 percent to finish at 11,069.01. (Reporting by Vivian\par Chu, Pedro Nicolaci da Costa, Gene Ramos, Manuela Badawy and Alden\par Bentley).\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040126e01q0008e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
