\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Markets To Drift Friday  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2004\par 06:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Volatile Trading Highlights Lowers Mkts \par  \par DJIA          10623.18   loss 0.44         0.0% \par NASDAQ        2119.01    loss 23.45     dn 1.1% \par S&P 500       1143.94    loss 3.67      dn 0.3% \par Dow Future    10599.00   loss 10.00     dn 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1532.00    loss 2.50      dn 0.2% \par S&P Future    1142.00    loss 1.25      dn 0.1% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.97% down 0.07 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Stocks drifted lower in choppy trading Thursday as investors sifted through economic reports and corporate earnings. Eastman Kodak surged on restructuring news. Treasurys rallied on the stock losses.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Tech stocks lost momentum for a second day amid weakness in the telecommunications and chip sectors, while bargain-hunting investors snapped up shares of American Airlines parent AMR Corp. following several analyst upgrades.  \par \par \uc2 At least some of the losses were due to profit taking, said Brian G. Belski, market strategist for Piper Jaffray & Co., but the emotions of earnings season make it difficult to predict trends.  \par \par \uc2 "You are seeing some rotation (away from tech), but I don't think it's a sea-change," Belski said. "One day doesn't make a trend. So my advice is, don't overreact."  \par \par \uc2 The index of leading economic indicators rose 0.2% in December, matching analysts' forecasts. The index, considered a predictor of future economic activity, rose on relatively strong consumer spending and indications that the job market is improving.  \par \par \uc2 Overall, corporate profits for the fourth quarter are expected to be 20% higher than a year ago, but some analysts say the market may be headed for a pause once earnings season is over.  \par \par \uc2 Eastman Kodak estimated a wide range for 2004 earnings as it turns its focus to digital photography. The company plans a 20% reduction in its work force and the overall cost-cutting plan helped lift its stock 13% to its highest level in seven months.  \par \par \uc2 Stronger demand for personal computers helped Microsoft boost sales of its signature Windows and Office products, increasing fiscal second-quarter revenue by 19%. However, earnings for the three-month period fell 17% as the company took a huge charge for stock-based compensation.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys rose steeply Thursday as buying by foreign central banks and investors, still making amends for betting against the market earlier this month, more than offset another round of healthy economic data.  \par \par \uc2 Both the five-year and 10-year notes exited active trade below their respective key resistance levels of 3.0% and 4.0%. That reignited speculation that that a wave of buying from mortgage investors, who need to rebalance their portfolios amid lower rates, could be just around the corner. If mortgage investors enter the market in large numbers, the yield on the 10-year note would likely move substantially lower.  \par \par \uc2 "With the 10-year (note) back below 4%, you renew those concerns," said John Canavan, bond market strategist at Stone & McCarthy in Princeton, N.J., who like many in the market sees a yield in the area of 3.90% as a potential trigger for mortgage-related buying of Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Traders kept a watchful eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to gauge future demand, particularly from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows have been enormous so far this month and a weaker dollar (Thursday) is putting a consistent bid back into the Treasury market," said John Spinello, fixed-income strategist at Merrill Lynch in New York.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Shares Edge Higher \par  \par USD-Yen             106.12       gain  0.07    up 0.08% \par AUD-USD             0.7664       loss  0.0016  dn 0.2% \par Nikkei 225          11134.00     gain  133.3   up 1.2% \par Hang Seng           13750.58     gain  180.10  up 1.3% \par JGB Yield           1.3050% down 0.0050 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)-- Tokyo stocks inched up Friday morning on demand for technology and bank issues.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early trading was capped by Wall Street's weakness. But prices then crept up as investors picked up technology issues such as Advantest and Tokyo Electron, and banks Mizuho, Mitsubishi Tokyo and UFJ, which ended Friday's morning session higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar remained weaker against the yen after it fell in New York. The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  briefly dipped to under Y106.00, within striking distance of the three-year lows reached earlier in the month of around 105.75 yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan believes that sharp \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuations are undesirable, and will continue to monitor the market closely for volatility, the vice finance minister for international affairs said Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese business activity slowed in November for the first time in four months, government data showed Friday, as several sectors pulled back from previous sharp gains and unseasonably warm weather took a toll on retail sales.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NAB shares "fell off a cliff", one broker said, after Standard & Poor's downgraded the outlook of Australia's biggest bank to negative from neutral late in the session.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Shares Seen Slightly Lower \par  \par Euro-USD            1.2706      loss  0.0008  dn 0.06% \par Stlg-USD            1.8430      loss  0.0012  dn 0.07% \par USD-Franc           1.2335      gain  0.0003  up 0.01% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European share prices are likely to start slightly lower, with prices of government bonds a shade higher. The euro is set to hold at around $1.2700.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European share prices are likely to open little changed to slightly lower, especially in technology, after Thursday's Nasdaq-led declines on Wall Street.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London's IG Index is calling the German and French markets slightly lower, with the London FTSE up a few points.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks tracked higher Thursday, with the healthcare sector outperforming as Novartis and Sanofi impressed with profit reports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Patent expiry for pharmaceutical companies is becoming less of an issue and the healthcare sector's sales growth is poised to reaccelerate after a three-year decline, said one London-based pharmaceuticals analyst.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CSFB strategists said they expect more downgrading of earnings numbers over the next few weeks as analysts update their models to reflect the weakness of the U.S. dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In our opinion investors are prepared for downgrading of this sort and should take it in their stride," they said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Porsche paces a light corporate reporting schedule for Friday in Europe.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds are likely to extend gains slightly after closing mostly higher Thursday, as a range-bound market tracks the euro's moves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market mainly focused on the euro during Thursday's session, paying little attention to lower-than-expected U.S. jobless claims, analysts said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Initial jobless claims fell by 1,000 in the week ended Jan. 17, well below expectations of a 2,000 rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market reaction was subdued because participants are waiting for January nonfarm payrolls data, after a weaker-than-expected reading in December, said Neil Cooper, bond analyst at CSFB.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People want to know whether it (the nonfarm payroll figure) was a one-time aberration or if there is something more substantial going on in terms of a very marked jobless recovery," said Cooper.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday, the macroeconomic agenda is fairly empty, with no major data from the euro zone or the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro opens the Friday session just over $1.2700, holding much of its gains from Thursday, when it came to within 1 1/2 cents of its record high of around $1.29.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Three days of losses have put expectations of a sustained correction for the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the back burner. Investors are growing less leery by the day of potential action by euro-zone officials to counter euro strength.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People hoping for a deeper euro correction have run out of patience," said Sean Callow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at IDEAglobal, a financial market research firm in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The prospect of a cut in interest rates by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely, while "it's a bit hard to believe that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is going to happen" before the euro breaks through the highs hit last Monday and approaches $1.30, Callow added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Patrick Brodie, chief dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. in New York, said little of the recent official commentary out of the euro zone has been supportive for the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But uncertainty about the outcome of the Feb. 6-7 meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations will keep investors wary about gunning for new lows in the dollar, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The general feeling is that this market is going to remain very choppy until we get to the G7, probably with a bias toward dollar weakness," said Brodie.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Friday, Jan 23: UK GDP, Retail Sales \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0430/0930 U.K.    4Q Preliminary Gross Domestic Product \par 0430/0930 U.K.    Dec Retail Sales (seen +0.4% MM) \par    N/A    U.S.    No economic data scheduled. \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181; dennis.baker@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Porsche (POR3.XE): 1H Earnings \par Consensus net profit (DJ, 9 analysts): EUR141M (EUR102M) \par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 9 analysts): EUR247M (EUR186M) \par Consensus sales: EUR2.94B (EUR2.21B) \par Note: Jump in profit is due to an easy comparable period, with Cayenne just entering the market a year ago. \par  \par Autoroutes du Sud de la France (18425.FR): 4Q/FY Revenue \par Consensus sales: (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR504M (EUR471.8M) \par Note: FY revenue seen at EUR2.241B. Estimates' tight range (EUR2.24B-EUR2.25B) belies lack of consensus over shares' attractiveness: SG Securities rates it a buy and says stock "still harbours speculative appeal;" Aurel says sell and BNP Paribas, CIC/ESN on the fence with neutral/hold ratings. \par OTHER SCHEDULED EVENTS: \par  \par BOC Group (BOX): AGM \par  \par ThyssenKrupp (TKA.XE): Shareholders Meeting \par  \par Quest For Growth (QFG.BT): 1H Earnings results \par  \par Bachem Holding AG (BANB.EB): FY Earnings \par  \par Get Group (GTG.LN): AGM \par  \par Ultraframe (UTF.LN): AGM \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 1011  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040123e01n0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\