\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Holds Ground Vs Yen But Lacks Momentum  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2004\par 06:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar recovered a little but remained generally unsteady against the yen in Asia Friday, with only caution over official bids by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  preventing the U.S. unit from slipping well below Y106.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback also held its ground against the euro as traders were wary of pushing the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher ahead of public appearances later in the day by senior European officials including European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Economist Otmar Issing.  \par \par \uc2 After falling as low as Y105.98 overnight in New York, the dollar recouped some losses and rose to an intraday high of Y106.28 in the morning as commercial banks placed bids ahead of the Tokyo fixing, when banks set their rates for the day.  \par \par \uc2 But that momentum soon tapered off, leaving the dollar stuck in a tight band just above Y106.00 for most of the day.  \par \par \uc2 Caution that the MOF may step in to stem the yen's further rise supported the dollar's downside, but many traders say it is a matter of time before the greenback tests lows below Y106 again.  \par \par \uc2 "Japanese monetary authorities stand ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  any time, but they'll have a hard time because the trend remains bearish for the dollar," a Japanese brokerage trader said "There's clearly more interest to go short on the dollar now."  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y106.17, above Y106.00 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y106.82 late Thursday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2703, lower than Thursday's $1.2711 in New York and $1.2672 in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y134.86, edging up from Y134.79 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y135.43 in Tokyo late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.8426, down from $1.8454 in New York but higher than $1.8333 in Tokyo Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 As if the market needed reminding, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  point man Zembei Mizoguchi repeated his usual verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lines, saying the ministry was "watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely" to act against big swings in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 But such well-worn remarks may not be enough to limit speculative moves ahead of the Feb. 6-7 meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations' finance ministers and central bankers in Boca Raton, Florida.  \par \par \uc2 The dominant view is that the G7 will stick to the phrase used in its last communique calling for "more flexibility in exchange rates," since expected calls from European officials for changes in this wording would be opposed by the U.S. and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 The more likely target of speculation will be \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance. Some players believe speculative yen buying may accelerate on the view that Tokyo may have to put the brakes on its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the run-up to the G7 to avoid criticism from its counterparts. In particular, European countries wary of the euro's rise may criticize Tokyo for not bearing the burden of the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 "We'll probably see the dollar testing new lows toward Y105.50, with choppy moves expected ahead of the G7 meeting," said Tohru Sasaki, J.P. Morgan. "The Y107 zone may be out of sight for some time," he said.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, market attention is focused on what European officials such as Issing and Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke will have to say about the euro's strength in their speeches later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 But many traders don't expect their comments to trigger any sharp decline in the euro, since recent comments by eurozone officials have reinforced the view that the ECB won't resort to market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or cut interest rates any time soon.  \par \par \uc2 -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040123e01n0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
