\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro down on warning of possible ECB rate cut.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2004\par 22:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 23 (Reuters) - The euro fell 1 percent against the dollar on Friday after a diplomatic source told Reuters that euro zone ministers attending a Group of Seven meeting next month will say that further strength in the euro could cause the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell from around $1.2685 after the comments, reaching a session low $1.2567, a loss of 0.7 percent from Thursday's New York close. Late Friday afternoon in New York, the euro traded around $1.2586.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the euro's fall accelerated as stop-loss orders were triggered around the $1.2600 level, which helped spark a round of dollar-buying against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In London, a European diplomatic source told Reuters that current euro zone monetary policy supports growth, but that external shocks, including those coming from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, could reduce growth.  \par \par \uc2 "This does not mean that monetary policy reacts directly to exchange rates but rather to the effects of exchange rates on growth and inflation," the source told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 Uppermost in the market's mind is what G7 industrial nations' finance ministers will say about dollar weakness when they meet in Florida on Feb. 6 and 7.  \par \par \uc2 Before the comments, the euro was already lower against the dollar, as investors took profits on the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp rise earlier in the week.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro fell on these Reuters headlines. The suggestion that the ECB is nearing a place at which it would say that further appreciation of the euro from current levels would be enough to trigger a monetary policy reaction is significant for the exchange rate," said Jason Bonanca, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "If the ECB turns to a more dovish tone, that means there is less of a divergence with the Fed, meaning the ECB is willing to do a little reflation of their own, and that would be bad for the euro against the dollar," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The market did not react to U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's pronouncement on Bloomberg radio that the United States backs a strong dollar with rates set by market forces in reflection of economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "There was some profit taking ahead of G7. It is coming up in two weeks and there is a real concern because no one wants to be late to that party in case G7 comes out and says something dollar-provocative," said Ralph DelZenero, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank One in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 MIXED SIGNALS  \par \par \uc2 Even though the euro suffered a sell-off, it was still up more than 2-1/2 cents against the dollar this week.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the roller-coaster ride for the euro over the last few weeks, which saw it drop 5 cents from a record high near $1.29 on Jan. 12, is caused by mixed signals coming from euro zone policy makers.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief economist Otmar Issing reiterated on Thursday the bank's worries about sharp movements in the euro.  \par \par \uc2 He said in an interview with Reuters on Friday that tax increases and one-time factors are expected to push euro zone inflation up 0.3 percent in early 2004, but euro strength should dampen price gains.  \par \par \uc2 ECB policymakers have stopped short of mapping out how they would reduce volatility and curb euro strength, with no strong indication that they would use interest rates or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an option.  \par \par \uc2 French Finance Minister Francis Mer said in Davos on Friday that the strong euro is starting to hurt corporate profits and may dent business confidence in Europe.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.46 percent to 106.48 yen on the day and recovered from a near three-year low of 105.76 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The market remains on guard for possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell to 133.47 yen but traded around 134.18 at the end of the New York afternoon. The dollar gained more than 1 percent in value against the Swiss franc, sterling, and Canadian dollar. (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040123e01n0066y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
