\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar may hit more turbulence before G7 - analysts.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2004\par 06:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 23 (Reuters) - The dollar, which over the past two weeks has gone through its most volatile swings in recent months, may encounter even more turbulence ahead of an upcoming Group of Seven meeting, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been most volatile against the euro so far, bouncing more than five percent from last week's record lows before erasing two-thirds of those gains, but analysts urge caution in dollar/yen rates as well.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was slightly firmer against the yen in early Friday trading on wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, but it remained within striking distance of last week's three-year lows of around 105.70 yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , totalling some 20 trillion yen ($188.6 billion) last year and seen topping five trillion yen already this month, has slowed the dollar's decline and kept option prices relatively cheap, particularly dollar put options with lower strike prices - the right to sell dollars at a lower level.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts urge caution ahead of the February 6-7 meeting of G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs.  \par \par \uc2 "Now is not the time to sell options," said Naomi Fink, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 For one thing, she said, Japanese authorities could tone down their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shortly before the G7 meeting, given their desire to put Europeans, who have been critical of Japan's rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, on their side at the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  talks.  \par \par \uc2 DUBAI DEJA VU?  \par \par \uc2 Prior to the previous G7 meeting in Dubai in September, the consensus among market players had put the limit for the dollar's decline at 115 yen - the line in the sand at which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be aimed.  \par \par \uc2 But as the authorities shied away from the market just before the meeting and stayed out until the end of September, the dollar swiftly broke through the long-standing support level.  \par \par \uc2 To be sure, most analysts do see a difference between the Dubai gathering and the upcoming meeting in Florida, given growing worries in Europe about the euro's strength.  \par \par \uc2 "At the time, it was only Japan that wanted to halt the dollar's fall. But this time, Europe is in a similar position," said Mitsuru Sahara, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing at UFJ Bank. "Besides, having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily already, there is no point in suddenly trying to keep a low profile now."  \par \par \uc2 So far, that appears to be the majority view in the options market.  \par \par \uc2 Implied volatilities - a measure of option prices that reflects how much spot rate fluctuation the market expects - were near the lower end of their ranges over the past several months, despite the spot dollar's proximity to new lows.  \par \par \uc2 At 0405 GMT, with the dollar at 106.17/22 yen, implied volatility for the at-the-money one-week option stood at 8.25/9.25 percent, down a quarter point from late Thursday. One-month stood at 9.1/9.6 percent.  \par \par \uc2 More conspicuously, risk reversals - the price difference in equally out-of-the-money puts and calls - were only slightly skewed in favour of dollar puts.  \par \par \uc2 Because exporters in Japan, which persistently runs a huge trade surplus, need to hedge their dollar income, dollar puts are almost always more expensive than calls and risk reversals often spike up above 3.0 points when the dollar plunges to new lows.  \par \par \uc2 But one-month 25-delta risk reversals stood at 0.65/0.85 in favour of dollar puts, near the low end of recent ranges and surprisingly low in view of the spot dollar level. "If anything, people who are buying options now tend to be those speculating on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and are buying the topside strike prices like 108 yen," said Ryosei Ishida, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing at Mizuho Corporate Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Fink at BNP Paribas warned against leaning too much on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  expectations, saying the dollar could break below 105 yen sometime near the G7 meeting or slightly afterwards.  \par \par \uc2 All the more worrying was the large amount of so-called exotic options that would be severly affected by - and would exacerbate - such a fall in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Ishida at Mizuho said one factor depressing volatilities now was hedging by market-makers who had sold their clients so-called knock-out options with barrier prices near 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 These contracts offer the clients a way of buying options at a low price but can backfire when the spot rate breaks the barrier prices and renders the options worthless.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, the market-maker banks, which must sell short-dated options to hedge against the high rate of time-decay of the knock-outs, would also have to reverse direction and buy back those hedges once the knock-outs disappear.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=106.05 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040123e01n00232\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
