\par\par \b \uc2 OFF THE RUN: Others Could Take Up  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Tsy Slack  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael S. Derby  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2004\par 20:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appetite for U.S. government bonds is continuing with unabated gusto, providing what observers say is a critical source of support for the market.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, all the buying by foreign central banks has been so strong, it's helping keep a market afloat that should instead be wilting in the face of an increasingly bright economic outlook, most strategists agree.  \par \par \uc2 But at least one bank, Goldman Sachs, takes a slightly different tack. They acknowledge \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying is important, but say foreigners are in general the more important variable, given the U.S.'s massive capital account shortfall with the rest of the world.  \par \par \uc2 Regardless of who's right, foreign buying of Treasurys is being fueled by a falling dollar, the descent of which has been so steep that it's spooked Asian central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying dollars and selling their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the tide. This gives them massive holdings of dollars which they need to reinvest somewhere, most typically by buying U.S. Treasurys and to a lesser degree agency debt. Analysts reckon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the largest foreign holder of Treasurys, totaled some 20 trillion yen in 2003, and Y6 trillion in the new year alone.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the Federal Reserve said that in the latest week its holdings of Treasurys and agencies soared by $22.2 billion, of which $19.3 billion was in government bonds. Wrightson ICAP analysts called the weekly gain "extraordinary."  \par \par \uc2 The Fed holds a substantial amount of Treasurys and agency debt in custody for other foreign central banks, although those banks are also able to buy and then hold Treasurys through private market sources as well.  \par \par \uc2 The buying has come at an opportune moment for the market, in that optimism for better economic growth this year is a deep and widely-held sentiment. Normally, that would be toxic for Treasurys, but the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying has held the market in.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Critical Buying \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign buying is "critical, it's probably the biggest driver" of the Treasury market recently, said Michael Ryan, strategist with UBS in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Goldman Sachs chief economist Bill Dudley, in a research note, argued the presence of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying is overblown. "We conclude that the US Treasury market would not be hurt very badly by a drop in such purchases," he noted. "Foreign investors as a group would still have to recycle the U.S. current account deficit back into dollar assets."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citing recently released Treasury Department data, Dudley said Japan is the major player in such purchases. "If Japan were not to keep buying, for example, then other foreign investors would be forced to recycle the dollars," he wrote.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Only if these foreign investors had strongly different preferences for U.S. dollar assets or if U.S. domestic investors could not be easily induced to increase their holding of Treasuries as relative prices adjusted, would the Treasury market be hit sharply."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, it's probably more important to keep in mind that Goldmans' somewhat contrarian take does nothing to diminish the role foreign interests play in supporting the government market. And in any case, the market is depending more than ever on foreign buyers, with the government running massive deficits, and set to do so for years to come.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When trying to divine where this is all heading, bond strategists say the question can only be answered by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Once the dollar finds a bottom, if central banks are satisfied the dollar won't decline anymore or the rate of decline is more acceptable, you would expect the central banks to back off," said Ward McCarthy, of consulting firm Stone & McCarthy Research Associates, in Princeton, N.J.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Michael S. Derby writes about markets, the economy and the Federal Reserve for Dow Jones Newswires)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     -Michael S. Derby, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4192; \par     michael.derby@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040123e01n0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
