\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Suffers Third Straight Day of Losses  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,091 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2004\par 22:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar continued to erase last week's gains on Thursday, with the euro climbing to within a 1 1/2 cents of its record high around $1.29.  \par \par \uc2 Three days of losses have put expectations of a sustained correction for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back burner, with investors growing less leery by the day of potential action by euro-zone officials to counter euro strength.  \par \par \uc2 "People hoping for a deeper euro correction have run out of patience," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAglobal, a financial market research firm in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The prospect of a cut in interest rates by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely, while "it's a bit hard to believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to happen" before the euro breaks through the highs hit last Monday and approaches $1.30, Callow added.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, market perceptions have shifted significantly since ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet's warning last week about "brutal" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves helped send the dollar on a five-cent rally against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 In the wake of his speech, other euro-zone officials have presented a much more mixed message about concern over the strength of the euro and its potential threat to the region's economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB reiterated in its January monthly bulletin published Thursday that it is "concerned about excessive exchange-rate moves."  \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed little inclination of changing its interest rate outlook, saying, "The current stance of monetary policy is appropriate to preserve price stability over the medium term."  \par \par \uc2 Patrick Brodie, chief dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. in New York, said little of the recent official commentary out of the euro zone has been supportive for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But uncertainty about the outcome of the Feb. 6-7 meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations will keep investors wary about gunning for new lows in the dollar, he added.  \par \par \uc2 "The general feeling is that this market is going to remain very choppy until we get to the G7, probably with a bias toward dollar weakness," said Brodie.  \par \par \uc2 While European officials will probably push for a change in the G7's last communique calling for "more flexibility in exchange rates" to ensure that the euro doesn't continue to bear the brunt of the adjustment to the falling dollar, that's unlikely given the expected opposition from the U.S. and Japan, say analysts.  \par \par \uc2 Callow of IDEAglobal said he doesn't expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to back down on its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen from rising too quickly and threatening the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the G7 is going to have to agree to disagree in terms of the role of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is estimated to have spent a whopping 6 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this month to keep the dollar around the 106 yen level.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  briefly dipped below that level Wednesday, falling nearly a yen to around 105.98 yen, within striking distance of the three-year lows reached earlier in the month around 105.75 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar appeared to recover back above 106 yen without any official support.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's strength followed a report by \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showing that capital inflows into the country rose to 2.2 trillion yen last week, the highest ever since records started in April 2002.  \par \par \uc2 "Put that on top of Japan's trade surplus (with the U.S.), and the BOJ has too strong a tide to swim against," said Callow.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday afternoon, the dollar was trading around 106.00 yen, below 106.92 yen late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.2711, up from $1.2623 late Wednesday in New York. It was slightly lower against the yen, at 134.79 yen from 135.07 yen. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar fell to 1.2338 francs from 1.2413 francs, while sterling rose to $1.8454 from $1.8297.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the market largely ignored generally positive data out of the U.S., including a decline of 1,000 jobless claims in the week ended Jan. 17 and a 0.2% rise in the Conference Board's composite index of leading indicators in December. The data are unlikely to change market expectations that the Federal Reserve would continue to keep interest rates steady well into the year.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Bank of Canada governor David Dodge stoked expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would further ease interest rates to counter the impact of the strong Canadian dollar on the local economy.  \par \par \uc2 "What the appreciation (of the Canadian dollar) means is that, as we look ahead, we're going to have to rely more on domestic demand rather than foreign demand, certainly more than one would have anticipated given the strength of the world economy, and that's the emphasis we've put in this report," said Dodge after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released its monetary policy report update.  \par \par \uc2 The report, which downgraded growth and inflation forecasts for Canada, reinforced analyst expectations for another 25 basis point rate cut from the Bank of Canada, bank, a repeat of last Tuesdays decision to ease the target rate to 2.50%.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the Canadian dollar managed to post a modest gain Thursday, with the U.S. dollar falling to C$1.2959 from C$1.3000 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real also rose after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly decided late Wednesday to leave interest rates unchanged, interrupting a seven-month monetary easing trend.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains came despite another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The real traded around 2.839 real to the dollar late Thursday, versus 2.845 real the day before.  \par \par \uc2 However, the Mexican peso fell for the second straight day, with the dollar breaking above 11.00 pesos for the first time since Jan. 5.  \par \par \uc2 The peso has been in retreat since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced plans Tuesday to sell $45 million a day between Feb. 2 and April 30 to slow the increase in reserves. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had appreciated sharply going into the announcement, and some investors are now unwinding their positions.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Don Curren in Toronto, Adriana Brasileiro in Rio de Janeiro, and Anthony Harrup in Mexico City contributed to this story.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040122e01m0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\