\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro higher vs dollar,  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  policy responses weighed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2004\par 10:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 22 (Reuters) - The euro built modest gains on the dollar on Thursday as doubts grew that finance leaders from the Group of Seven (G7) industrialised nations would unite to put a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they meet in February.  \par \par \uc2 European leaders are expected to press for a joint statement from the G7 to help take some of the burden of the weak dollar off the euro. And European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) members have recently attempted to talk down the euro's strength.  \par \par \uc2 However, Wednesday's comments from ECB Governing Council member Nout Wellink and Chief Economist Otmar Issing were more relaxed about the strength of the euro, which is now trading about two cents below last week's record high on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "What is notable is a shift in the ECB stance. Wellink's comments were very telling," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London.  \par \par \uc2 "He seemed less concerned about the euro. The recent move lower in the euro has reduced some of the ECB's concern and this opens the euro to further strength."  \par \par \uc2 At 0900 GMT the euro was up 0.12 percent on the day against the dollar at $1.2646, versus Wednesday's close around $1.2630.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit all-time highs on the dollar around $1.2900 last week, but fell to just above $1.2300 earlier this week on the back of critical comments from European policymakers including the ECB.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated its concern about excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility in January's monthly bulletin, which came out on Thursday, but the euro held its ground after the information was released.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was roughly steady at 106.76 versus the dollar and 134.94 the euro as the spectre of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept a lid on yen buying.  \par \par \uc2 GLOBAL \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  POLICIES  \par \par \uc2 Investors are now awaiting a string of public appearances by key economic policy makers over the next two days, looking for hints on how Europe, United States and Japan will act towards dollar weakness in the future.  \par \par \uc2 "European officials are complaining about the euro's rally, but I really think those complaints are also against \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "European officials must be furious that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is actually exaggerating the euro's rise, since dollar-strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  indirectly puts upward pressure on the euro."  \par \par \uc2 So far, Washington has signalled it would support a hands-off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect export competitiveness. This leaves the Europeans shouldering much of the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, who has repeatedly said he supported a strong dollar whose value should be set by markets, is due to hold a news conference on tax issues at 1815 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 ECB's Issing is due to speak at 1900 GMT during the World Economic Forum in Switzerland's mountain resort of Davos.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet is scheduled to address the Davos forum on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said market uncertainty about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy decisions in the run-up to the February 6-7 G7 summit meant a period of volatile trading in the next two weeks.  \par \par \uc2 However, market worries over the U.S.'s ability to finance its current account deficit meant the dollar would remain under pressure in the longer term.  \par \par \uc2 Japan, for its part, has given no sign of easing up on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters on Thursday that the Japanese authorities were ready to step in and take action if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates overshoot.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($187.2 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and market players estimate it has already offloaded another six trillion in 2004.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040122e01m0037h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
