\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar weakens vs majors on pre-G7 jitters.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2004\par 07:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 22 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Thursday on growing doubt that Group of Seven (G7) finance leaders will take a stand against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness when they meet next month.  \par \par \uc2 European officials' concerns about the euro's rise were no longer having the sort of impact they did last week, when the dollar bounced by nearly five percent, leaving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back within sight of life lows.  \par \par \uc2 "European officials started screaming about the euro's strength, but looking at what happened after the G7 in Dubai, I don't think they want to mention anything explicitly this time," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 At the Dubai meeting, G7 ministers called for more flexible exchange rates, a call the market perceived was aimed at Asia but which, in the weeks that followed, drove the dollar down against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "It was a huge political mistake by European officials," said Umemoto.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said choppy trade would likely continue as the market sought hints about whether, if at all, policymakers from the G7 industrial countries nations would express a concerted view on the dollar's weakness at their meeting in Florida on February 6-7. The euro has moved wildly against the dollar, dipping to a one-month low of $1.2350 late last week, before coming back to around $1.2670 by 0601 GMT. That is within two percent of its life highs near $1.29 set last week.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar weakened against the tweek's three-year lows around 105.70 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Persistent concerns about U.S. fundamentals also had a hand in softening the greenback, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "From a long-term perspective, nothing has really changed in terms of the U.S. current account deficit or expectations that the dollar will eventually head lower," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the interbank desk at Trust & Custody Services Bank.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  BLUES  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for its part, has given no sign of easing up on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - employed to sap the export-crimping strength of the yen - ahead of the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters on Thursday that the Japanese authorities were ready to step in and take action if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates overshoot.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said he had met Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui earlier in the day. "I asked for his assistance at what will be my first G7 as a minister," Tanigaki told reporters. He declined to comment on what was discussed.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said one factor supporting a view that G7 ministers may not make a unified stand on the dollar was growing frustration among European officials over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "European officials are complaining about the euro's rally, but I really think those complaints are also against \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "European officials must be furious that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is actually exaggerating the euro's rise, since dollar-strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  indirectly puts upward pressure on the euro."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record 20 trillion yen ($187.2 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and market players estimate it has already offloaded another six trillion in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, the market was awaiting a flurry of public appearances by European and U.S. policymakers including European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Jean-Claude Trichet and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chief economist, Otmar Issing.  \par \par \uc2 Both were due to speak at the annual World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland. ($1=106.86 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040122e01m002mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
