\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ buys Y5 trln of MOF bonds to fund  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2004\par 03:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about five trillion yen ($46.8 billion) of foreign bonds to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month to finance its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 By temporarily selling foreign bonds held as official reserves, the government raised funds needed for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  using a framework hastily set up to avoid a funding crunch for its efforts to stem the yen's export-crimping gains.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ accounts data showed its "receivables under resales agreements" jumped to 10.7 trillion yen on January 20 from 6.1 trillion yen on January 10.  \par \par \uc2 Stripping out the BOJ's other transactions under resale agreements, such as some open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ's buying of foreign bonds from the ministry is seen at around five trillion yen, Totan Research chief economist Izuru Kato said.  \par \par \uc2 The data came as Japan's top financial diplomat reiterated that financial authorities remained ready to step in and take action if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates overshoot.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar-selling based on the U.S. twin deficits as a factor is a speculative move that does not reflect real conditions and is inappropriate," Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar sank nearly 10 percent against the yen last year, falling to 107 yen from near 119. It was last trading at 106.89 yen, up from a three-year low of 105.70 set last week.  \par \par \uc2 The move by the ministry to borrow funds stemmed from a draining of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  war chest.  \par \par \uc2 Normally the ministry borrows yen by selling financing bills to pay for its dollar-buying in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 But due to aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and early this year, market players think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing hit its limit imposed by parliament, forcing the ministry to seek stopgap measures.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and market players estimate it has already offloaded another six trillion in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry last year also moved to raise the borrowing limit to 140 trillion yen in the fiscal year starting next April from the current 79 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 The increase of 61 trillion yen, equivalent to about $570 billion, roughly matches the annual U.S. current account deficit. ($1=106.86 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040122e01m00134\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
