\par\par \b \uc2 Econ Adviser Tanaka: Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Policy May Change Next Year  \b0\par\par\uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2004\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan is "buying time" through its massive yen-selling campaign until the U.S. becomes more capable of attracting overseas funds, but Tokyo may have to steer away from its aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy if its goals are not met this year, an influential Japanese economist said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's underlying downtrend has been caused by a decrease in foreign capital inflows into the United States in the past three years, said Naoki Tanaka, an economic adviser to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. will likely attract more overseas funds this year as its economy achieves stronger growth, leading to more stable moves in the dollar/yen, Tanaka said in a speech at the Foreign Correspondents' Club of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at buying time until this happens, Tanaka said, warning that Tokyo may have to review its proactive policy if this scenario fails to materialize this year. "So this year is very important" for Japan's exchange rate policy, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a record Y20.05 trillion last year to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising and hurting exports, and is believed to have sold trillions more yen this month.  \par \par \uc2 Despite such efforts, the yen has risen nearly 11% against the dollar since the beginning of last August, raising questions about the effectiveness of the action.  \par \par \uc2 Many market players expect the dollar's downtrend to continue amid persistent concern over the huge U.S. budget and trade deficits.  \par \par \uc2 Tanaka countered this view, saying that room for further dollar depreciation is "very limited" since the greenback's purchasing power will be "maintained" at current levels on receding signs of inflation in the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 The huge U.S. current account deficit will be balanced out by the nation's "very huge" capital account surplus, Tanaka said, playing down the trade deficit's negative impact on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Tanaka was upbeat about the Japanese economy, saying that it will enter a "new phase" this year on increasing output and investment.  \par \par \uc2 While conceding that the Japanese economy still depends on export growth, Tanaka said the negative effect of the yen's rise against the dollar on the earnings of Japanese exporters would be limited.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese manufacturers have become "resilient" against yen strength as they changed their business structure to become less vulnerable to exchange rate swings, Tanaka said.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese government is worried that a higher yen would hurt the economy by making Japanese exporters' goods less competitive overseas, as well as depressing repatriated earnings.  \par \par \uc2 Tanaka currently heads the 21st Century Public Policy Institute, a think tank attached to Japan's big business lobby, Keidanren.  \par \par \uc2 He is viewed as having close ties with Koizumi and is known for his stint as former chair of the prime minister's postal system privatization panel.  \par \par \uc2 -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040122e01m0007l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
