\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-G7 hoping absent Asia comes to  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  party.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Mair  \par \uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2004\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 21 (Reuters) - When the finance ministers of the world's most powerful industrial nations meet in Florida next month, much discussion will centre on those who are not there.  \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks ignored the Group of Seven's call for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility last September, and continued to buy dollars to keep their competitive edge in export markets.  \par \par \uc2 Asia's refusal to accept a weaker dollar is causing pain for Europe. It puts upward pressure on the euro as one means of helping the U.S. current account gap to narrow. Asia's policies are also drawing election-year criticism from U.S politicians who say they give Asian countries unfair trade advantages.  \par \par \uc2 The problem for the rest of the world is that Asia - with its rising stockpile of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - can afford to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 And that leaves the G7 - the United States, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada - facing a dilemma of how to get Asia to accept some of the burden of a weaker dollar without creating new strength in the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "All that the G7 can do is to continue to emphasise that we need to work together, that policies need to be there to allow that widespread and smooth adjustment, and that flexibility on exchange rates is one of many policies," Westpac Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Robert Rennie said.  \par \par \uc2 Asia's reserves stood at $1.9 trillion at the end of 2003, having risen by one-third over the year as the region fought a weakening dollar. Analysts say the trend seems set to continue.  \par \par \uc2 Already in 2004, Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen is thought to have reached new records, the Bank of Japan has eased policy, and South Korea has cut a link between onshore and offshore markets to keep the won from rallying.  \par \par \uc2 DUBAI VOLATILITY  \par \par \uc2 At a September meeting in Dubai, G7 finance ministers called for greater flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . European officials later made it clear it was a call for Asia to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks subsequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  even more.  \par \par \uc2 "The immediate aftermath of Dubai and the volatility that statement arguably created probably means that the G7 officials will be somewhat more guarded in terms of the Boca Raton statement," Rennie said.  \par \par \uc2 While the yen has rallied almost seven percent against the dollar since Dubai, the euro has risen more than 10 percent.  \par \par \uc2 But other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have not followed the yen. Japan is relying on exports to drive its recovery, and does not want to lose competitiveness against other Asian exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Since the September meeting, the won has weakened about two percent, while the Taiwan dollar has risen about one percent and the Singapore dollar about two percent.  \par \par \uc2 "But with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  now drawing a line in the sand as well, there is a greater possibility this time around that more pressure will be put on Asian finance ministers," Lehman Brothers \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said in a report.  \par \par \uc2 YEN CONDUIT  \par \par \uc2 The question remains: how can the G7 influence economic policy in countries that are not members of the forum?  \par \par \uc2 "The mode in a way would be through the yen again," Standard Chartered Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Claudio Piron said.  \par \par \uc2 "That said, what we have seen from the Japanese in the past week has been very strong in terms of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the easing."  \par \par \uc2 Importantly, China has not loosened its grip on the closely managed yuan. Piron said Japan may be prepared to trade a stronger yen for a change in Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, as that would likely see other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen.  \par \par \uc2 "If they could get the Chinese to do that, that would be a worthy sacrifice in their eyes potentially," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Even in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the yen has sustained its rally, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has accelerated to stop it rising too far. At the moment, markets think 105 yen per dollar is the limit of Japan's tolerance, compared with 115 before the last G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Still, by the end of the year the G7 could get its way, but only because it might suit Asia to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength to deal with inflation and avoid the rising costs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts expect Asia to lead a resurgence in global growth in 2004 which, combined with artificially low exchange rates, would lead to rising local prices and pressure for tighter monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 "This will make Asian central banks more relaxed with regards to nominal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciations," said Simon Flint, Bank of America's senior Asia strategist.  \par \par \uc2 . (C) Reuters Limited 2004.   