\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: G7 And The Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 993 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2004\par 16:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After watching the dollar surge late last week behind a few utterances from European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, the European officials appeared to have turned the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market without an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -shot being fired.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Trichet's self-confidence no doubt soared along with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But at the end of the day as in by Tuesday, the new call from the ECB for a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market failed to inoculate the market from selling dollar. It was little more than an excuse to take profits by some large funds, and this, in turn, forced the rest of the buy-side food chain to cover dollar shorts.  \par \par \uc2 In other words, this move was still in the phase of a major trend where we would call it news chasing price. The level of importance of the news, a change in ECB tolerance for euro/dollar rise, was disproportionate to the change in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. In other words, the fundamentals that got euro/dollar to 1.2880 last week had not materially changed. The phase one expects when a major trend like the one we are in now has the characteristics of news driving price. In other words, the change in fundamentals is proportionate to the change in price suggesting sustainability (in this case a sustained dollar reversal higher).  \par \par \uc2 Organized and unified G7 response is a necessary but not sufficient condition for turning the dollar downtrend. But G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (dollar) concerns are anything but organized and unified. Much is being made of a Europe-Japan alliance to pressure the U.S. to back a G7 communique (Feb. 7) calling for a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market (i.e. dollar). This is simply not going to fly. First of all the Europe-Japan alliance is flawed. The Dubai communique from late September meant one thing for Europe and another for Japan...the call for more flexible exchange rates. For Europe, this meant burden sharing for the adjustment process the U.S. faces over its record current account deficit. Clearly for Europe this meant a lower dollar/yen, or more explicitly for Japan to allow dollar/yen to float. For Japan, this meant China should relax its dollar/yuan peg. For the U.S., this meant both \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less and China should revalue. Europe is not keen on a China-revaluation shock now.  \par \par \uc2 While both European and Japanese officials are eager to see the dollar steady to higher, what on earth can they say or do to get the Bush administration to embrace this in early February? It was comical to see Washington insiders last week drag out tired notions of pre-G7 horse trading over communique language as if G7 was nearing a major new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  accord. Did they forget that the Bush administration really does not care much for what best suits Germany and France given what happened over Iraq? And what is the rush? From the U.S. viewpoint, the weak dollar is just what the doctor ordered ... record current account deficit, need for jobs growth ahead of the election, monetary and fiscal policy tapped and inflation is low. Moreover, the dollar's decline has been orderly. As far as the likelihood of the U.S. to sign off on language calling for a stable dollar and suggesting some joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a policy option now...a fantasy. It is almost as absurd as insiders concluding the ECB is increasingly considering a rate cut to stem the rise in the euro. Sure, at some level in euro/dollar (and pace of appreciation) a rate cut will happen. But not now, not next week, not before the summer and not absent a dollar collapse.  \par \par \uc2 Even suggestions that the ECB is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in euro/dollar unilaterally is laughable. Without the U.S. involved the risk of unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is remote. And bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is equally unfounded.  \par \par \uc2 This begs the question whether euro/dollar powers higher (as in above 1.2880 high from last week) ahead of or after the G7 meeting Feb. 6-7? It looks like the market is ready to test the high from last week ahead of G7. And because no trend is only one-way, the meeting may prompt some profit taking..."buying the rumor selling the news". But there is still little standing in the way of steady dollar declines ahead. Trading to 1.30 before G7 is very possible, and surely post-G7. Think the more pressing question is, "What about dollar/yen?" The BoJ policy easing this week suggests some attempt by MoF to begin a trial separation in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  marriage. But far too soon to make this conclusion. That said, it does appear that the record dollar/yen buying by the BoJ for MoF earlier this month has caused a rethink (about 20-25% of all of 2003 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has been looking an awful lot like a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board of late. Japan is in effect dollarizing. MoF has ceded control over monetary policy. And official money supply data from Japan is trending lower. We think this is an unsustainable policy and MoF went "postal" this month with its $50 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is now finally coming to terms with its off-the-G7-charts behavior. Indeed we see a much lower dollar/yen in the wake of Boca Raton meeting as Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy stands out like a carbuncle on the face of G7.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.)  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040121e01l0001l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
