\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar retains recent gains, euro eyes Fin Mins.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2004\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 19 (Reuters) - The dollar kept a grip on recent gains against the euro and yen, supported by caution ahead of a euro zone finance ministers' meeting later on Monday after European policymakers talked it off record lows last week. The dollar staged its biggest one-day rise against the euro in five months on Friday, aided by data showing net foreign purchases of U.S. assets tripled to $87.6 billion in November from the previous month and by protests from euro zone policymakers about the pace of the euro's recent rise.  \par \par \uc2 With the market now waiting to see what Group of Seven Finance Ministers will say about the dollar's two-year decline when they meet in early February, any comments from euro zone politicians at Monday's meeting will be watched.  \par \par \uc2 "There's caution ahead of the Eurogroup finance ministers' meeting," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One in London.  \par \par \uc2 "Last week the comments from European officials were one of the triggers for this renaissance in the dollar and it seems likely that they will continue to reiterate that they would be opposed to a considerably stronger euro exchange rate."  \par \par \uc2 By 0845 GMT the dollar was steady on the day $1.2375 per euro after gaining two cents on Friday. It hit an all-time low of $1.2898 last Monday but rapidly rebounded after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials began expressing their concern.  \par \par \uc2 It was a quarter percent higher on the day at 106.91 yen, holding more than one yen up from a recent three-year low, with the market ever-wary of official Japanese yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar was also staging a strong rebound against the Australian and New Zealand dollars and was firm against sterling and the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 PRESSURE ON YEN TO RISE  \par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, dealers said trading was thin due to the U.S. holiday in honour of Martin Luther King Jr, but dollar/yen was weighed down by expectations that Japanese investors would sell the euro against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Some investors seem to have already started repatriating their overseas investments, so further upward pressure on the yen is likely to continue," said Tomoko Fujii, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Some major institutional investors said they took profits on euro-denominated bonds during the euro's rise to a record levels. The euro stood around 132.30 yen after dipping to a one-month low of 131.60 on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Over the longer term, analysts said there were few factors to push down the yen except yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  but they expected short-covering in dollar/yen positions in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 Data from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission showed on Friday that yen futures speculators extended net long positions to 60,602 contracts in the week ending January 13 from 56,834 contracts a week earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Helping to support the yen was a rise in Japanese stock prices, with the benchmark Nikkei stock average ending above 11,000 for the first time since late October.  \par \par \uc2 PRE-G7 VIGIL  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said it was too early to say the euro's uptrend had ended but Friday's U.S. inflows data had given pause for thought.  \par \par \uc2 "Steady inflows to U.S. assets mean less worries about the United States not being able to finance its massive current account deficit, which has been a major factor battering the dollar," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts noted that with little in the way of major U.S. data out this week, attention would focus on Tuesday's full Ecofin meeting of European finance ministers, along with a European parliamentary testimony of ECB chief economist Otmar Issing, and a World Economic Forum starting in Davos on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040119e01j001mm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
