\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steady vs majors on capital inflows data.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara   \par \uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2004\par 08:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 19 (Reuters) - The dollar kept most of its gains against major rivals on Monday on data showing steady capital inflows to U.S. assets and as the market speculated on the outcome of the upcoming Group of Seven (G7) meeting.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady against the euro around $1.2390 after notching its biggest one-day rise in five months on Friday, when it climbed to $1.2350 - a level not seen in a month.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market took heart from data showing that net foreign purchases of U.S. assets tripled to $87.6 billion in November from the previous month.   \par \par \uc2 "Steady inflows to U.S. assets mean less worries about the United States not being able to finance its massive current account deficit, which has been a major factor battering the dollar," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2 "That took some pressure off the dollar, especially as euro-zone officials have repeatedly voiced concerns about the euro's rapid rise versus the dollar."   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said it was too early to say that the euro's uptrend had ended, but they were on alert for further warnings against the euro's strength from a meeting of euro-zone finance ministers at 1800 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Among the latest remarks to come out of Europe, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Economist Otmar Issing said on Friday that the ECB was not indifferent to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.   \par \par \uc2 After Issing, ECB council member Guy Quaden said a further rise in the euro was not desirable.   \par \par \uc2 Amid the recent barrage of coments from euro-zone officials, the market was focused on how G7 policy-makers would react to the dollar's overall weakness when they meet in Florida on February 6-7.   \par \par \uc2 Views are divided, with many market participants believing that U.S. officials are satisfied with the dollar's weakness since it helps U.S. manufacturers.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow reiterating on Friday the Bush administration's strong dollar policy and that markets should set \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.   \par \par \uc2 PRESSURE ON YEN TO RISE   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was hardly changed against the yen, trading around 106.65 yen versus 106.70 in late U.S. trade.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said trading was thin due to the U.S. holiday in honour of Martin Luther King Jr., but the dollar/yen was weighed down by expectations that Japanese investors would sell the euro against the yen.   \par \par \uc2 "Some investors seem to have already started repatriating their overseas investments, so further upward pressure on the yen is likely to continue," said Tomoko Fujii, market and economic analysis director at Nikko Citigroup in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 Some major institutional investors said they took profits on euro-denominated bonds during the euro's rise to a record levels.   \par \par \uc2 "We have shifted a modest amount to euro bonds from dollar bonds," said Takeshi Furuichi, general manager at the finance and investment planning department of Nippon Life.   \par \par \uc2 He added that the amount was "tens of billions of yen" of the firm's two trillion yen ($18.7 billion) in foreign bond holdings.   \par \par \uc2 The euro stood around 132.40 yen , recovering from around 132 yen in late U.S. trade.   \par \par \uc2 Over the longer term, analysts said there were few factors set to push down the yen except \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they expect short-covering in dollar/yen positions in the near term.   \par \par \uc2 Data from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission showed on Friday that yen futures speculators extended net long positions to 60,602 contracts in the week that ended on January 13 from 56,834 contracts a week earlier.   \par \par \uc2 Helping to support the yen was a rise in Japanese stock prices, with the benchmark Nikkei stock average ending above 11,000 for the first time since late October. ($1=106.65 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040119e01j0016z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
