\par\par \b \uc2 COMEX gold ends short pre-holiday session lower.  \b0\par\par\uc2 552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2004\par 21:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 16 (Reuters) - COMEX gold fell to a one-month low in a choppy abbreviated session Friday, extending a shakeout as the dollar recovery against the euro accelerated and the appeal of safe-havens dimmed.  \par \par \uc2 Benchmark futures held to relatively narrow $5.50 range, after Thursday's $13.30 rout, before metals started closing at midday for Monday's U.S. Martin Luther King Jr. holiday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro's losses snowballed after a week of comments from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expressing worries about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 20 percent rise over the past year. But gold bulls were reluctant to sell aggressively before a long weekend.  \par \par \uc2 February gold settled down $1.70 at $407.00 an ounce. It plumbed its lowest price since Dec. 15 at $405.70, off a high of $411.20 hit in a brief rally after the open.  \par \par \uc2 Estimated volume was 48,000 contracts, compared with Thursday's frantic 109,039. The liquidation reduced open interest by 6,444 contracts.  \par \par \uc2 Graham Leighton, a vice president at Societe Generale, said the technical picture and economic fundamentals could be aligning negatively for gold, which could easily see $390 an ounce if $400 broke.  \par \par \uc2 "All we need to do now is get to the point here where we change market sentiment a little bit from the precious point of view and we could see these prices tumble fairly significantly," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The selling of dollars for gold lifted the February contract to a 15-year high at $431.50 last Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Gold prices went up 20 percent last year amid dollar diversification. With worries about the U.S. occupation of Iraq and al Qaeda attacks, speculators were holding one of the largest longs ever, betting that gold would be seen as the safest alternative to the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 However, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the rise of the yen, which is at its highest price in more than three years against the dollar. Many worry the ECB may start selling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or cut interest rates to stimulate its economy, and that has taken the steam out of the euro's record run.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell to $1.2374/77 Friday from the close at $1.2575/80. On Monday it reached a lifetime high of $1.2898, awarding European bullion buyers with extra \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  leverage.  \par \par \uc2 A commodity broker said the long-term gold outlook remained bullish, describing the pullback as a "big correction with a little influence from the euro."  \par \par \uc2 He concluded, "This is just a much-needed correction."  \par \par \uc2 Spot gold closed at $406.20/6.95, down from the late quote at $408.75/9.50 Thursday. London's afternoon fix was $408.40.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par March silver  bucked gold's selloff, rising 11.7 cents higher to $6.33,\par more than halving Thursday's 21.7-cent tumble. The range was $6.165 to\par $6.34. Spot silver        rose to $6.27/29 from $6.19/21. The fix was\par $6.205.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Silver hit a six-year high on Monday at $6.795.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NYMEX April platinum  rose $3 to $851.90 an ounce. Spot        closed\par at $853.00/858.00.\par March palladium  went up $4 to $219.65 an ounce. Spot        last\par fetched $213.00/218.00.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040116e01g003ca\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
