\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar climbs sharply, lifted by robust data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2004\par 18:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 16 (Reuters) - The dollar gained more than 1 percent against some of its major counterparts on Friday, drawing strong support from U.S. economic data showing foreigners were bigger-than-expected investors in U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 This week's barrage of comments from euro zone officials expressing concern about the recent surge in the value of the euro showed no signs of abating on Friday, fueling further dollar gains.  \par \par \uc2 Their comments also fanned speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cut rates at its next meeting in February to stem the euro's climb, analysts said. However, a euro zone monetary source told Reuters the ECB would use interest rate cuts only as a last resort.  \par \par \uc2 "If the ECB were concerned about volatility, they've done a fine job of introducing even more volatility," said Shaun Osborne, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto.  \par \par \uc2 "This does not mean that the underlying trend toward euro strength is reversing, but we could see the pace of the euro appreciation slow somewhat," he added.  \par \par \uc2 By midday in New York, the euro traded at $1.2378 , off 1.5 percent, and more than five cents lower from Monday's record highs just under $1.29. The euro fell about 1 percent against the yen to 132.31 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied against the yen to 106.87 yen , up 0.50 percent on the day, after having hit a low around 105.73.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the spike up for the dollar was fueled by a short-covering squeeze play rather than yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent heavily in the last year to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too far, too fast against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think they're (Japan) doing anything. They don't have to. All these are in the context of overall dollar-buying we've seen this week," said John McCarthy, director for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar rose 1.7 percent to 1.2666 francs . Sterling fell to $1.7952 , off 1.4 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR CHEERED BY U.S. DATA  \par \par \uc2 The dollar got an early boost after upbeat U.S. economic data showed a surprise rise in foreign purchases of U.S. assets. The Treasury Department said net foreign purchases of U.S. assets jumped to $87.6 billion in November, sharply higher than a revised $27.8 billion in October.  \par \par \uc2 "It was almost instantaneous. The private accounts showed a big turnaround," said Grant Wilson, vice president at Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh.  \par \par \uc2 Even a smaller-than-expected increase in U.S. industrial production did not dent the dollar's rally.  \par \par \uc2 Adding to the dollar's advance was a bullish reading on the University of Michigan's preliminary survey on consumer sentiment, which came in at 103.2 in January, the highest in more than three years.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, visiting the New York Stock Exchange, reiterated the Bush administration's strong dollar policy but said markets should set \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPE SPEAKS OUT AGAIN  \par \par \uc2 ECB Chief Economist Otmar Issing added to downward pressure on the euro, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not indifferent to the euro's strength. After Issing, ECB council member Guy Quaden said a further rise in the euro on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was not desirable.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a sudden shift in attitude in the sense that there is greater acceptance that the dollar is going to have a correction partly because of what may or may not happen at the G7 meeting," said Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco Group Ltd in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 The market is focused on how policy-makers from the G7 industrial countries will react to the dollar's weakness at their meeting in Florida on Feb. 6-7.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040116e01g0036e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
