\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-G7 faces headache in phrasing  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  communique.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Dolan  \par \uc2 888 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2004\par 15:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 16 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven rich nations looks some distance from collective action to lift a weak dollar but faces a headache in agreeing a public position on the matter.  \par \par \uc2 Three weeks before its top finance officials meet, the temptation may be to say nothing rather than risk a repeat of what some saw as a clumsy communique they issued in Dubai last September.  \par \par \uc2 This week's modest dollar recovery against the euro, if sustained, may also encourage the G7 - the United States, Japan, Germany, France, Britain, Italy and Canada - to bite its lip.  \par \par \uc2 But market experts warn that silence risks endorsing the steep dollar slide since Dubai and encouraging more of the same.  \par \par \uc2 "It's possible that difficulty in phrasing a statement means we see one of the least memorable G7 communiques of recent times," said Avinash Persaud, director of Global Asset Management's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund.  \par \par \uc2 "But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have a history of pushing until they get direct policy response," he said, adding that in the absence of such a response more dollar losses were likely.  \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and central bankers, who have a 20-year history of agreeing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on markets to correct extreme movements in the world's major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meet in Boca Raton, Florida on February 6 and 7. The G7 deputies, dubbed "sherpas", meet in Brussels on January 26 to prepare the agenda.  \par \par \uc2 Mindful of past bouts of coordinated G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the last being to buy euros in September 2000, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and analysts pore over every nuance of the Group's statements.  \par \par \uc2 This time around \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely, chiefly because the U.S. is seen to be happy with a status quo where a weak dollar helps to ease its enormous trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve sees no inflation impulse from a lower \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Treasury is unlikely to agree to anything that would see a substantial dollar rise during a presidential election year when jobs and economic growth are critical.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. may urge Europe to lower interest rates as a way of controlling the euro. Europe and Japan will probably point to the U.S. budget deficit as a root of the dollar's problem. The U.S. will counter by saying the deficit was responsible for its economic recovery and the global rebound.  \par \par \uc2 Arguments are likely to be circular and show little sign of coming to a conclusion if all seven should buy dollars right now. But the G7 statement, as in Dubai, can still make big waves and this is the conundrum for the Florida meeting.  \par \par \uc2 PERVERSE IMPACTS  \par \par \uc2 Dubai's statement had a clear intent but a perverse impact.  \par \par \uc2 Implicitly accepting the need for a weaker dollar to ease the ballooning U.S. budget and current account deficits, the G7 tried to urge non-G7 Asian economic giants such as China and South Korea to stop fixing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so tightly to the falling U.S. unit.  \par \par \uc2 But with these nations unlikely to budge soon and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  still committed to unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow any yen rise, the dollar slumped against the line of least resistance.  \par \par \uc2 A freely-floating euro, whose underlying economies were the weakest of the seven, soared more than 10 percent on the dollar since Dubai. "I suspect next month's G7 could give us much the same as Dubai, but there may be some attempt to make the wording on more flexible exchange rates more explicit to Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mark Cliffe, Chief Economist at ING Financial.  \par \par \uc2 Yet, G7 texts rarely mention specific countries. Mention of a need for dollar flexibility in Asia in general would be vetoed by Japan, fearful it could signal a rout in dollar/yen.  \par \par \uc2 So, the U.S. will want to avoid any wording that could trigger a dollar rally, Japan is wary of anything referring to Asia and saying nothing may be deemed as too risky.  \par \par \uc2 The only other possibility is Europe gets agreement on a form of words discouraging extreme movements.  \par \par \uc2 After hesitating for many weeks after Dubai, euro zone governments and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have recently expressed concern that rapid euro gains much beyond highs near $1.30 will undermine the region's fragile export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has retreated as speculation grew that more euro gains could be met by unilateral ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell euros or an ECB interest rate cut. Euro group finance ministers meeting on Monday may nuance their position favouring a "strong and stable" euro with a line about deploring excess volatility.  \par \par \uc2 The euro zone position is also likely to get a strong airing in Florida at joint press briefings from ECB chief Jean-Claude Trichet, European monetary affairs commissioner Pedro Solbes and current euro group chairman Charlie McCreevy.  \par \par \uc2 All this will be unilateral, however, and outside an agreed G7 text. Getting words into the communique may not be as easy.  \par \par \uc2 "Warning about excess volatility could be dangerous as it may signal a commitment to action at some point," said Julian Callow, Chief European Economist at Barclays Capital.  \par \par \uc2 But with little clarity on what constitutes "excess volatility", the phrase may be the one G7 can use without being seen to be hopelessly divided or burying its head in the sand.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040116e01g002p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
