\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro losses snowball, BoJ keeps lid on yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2004\par 14:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 16 (Reuters) - The euro's losses gathered speed on Friday, boosting the yen and prompting the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hit three-year highs against the dollar before being turned back by suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BoJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 This week's barrage of comments from euro zone officials expressing concern about the euro's 20 percent rise on the dollar over the past year showed no signs of abating on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 On the contrary, analysts said an increasing number of comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) officials added weight to earlier criticism from euro zone politicians.  \par \par \uc2 "This a really significant change. We are starting to hear from the ECB. Its officials are getting extremely vocal while before we have had politicians and business leaders," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London.  \par \par \uc2 "In the run-up to the G7 (Group of Seven) meeting this takes even more significance...It increases expectations that Europe will be pressing for a strong statement in the G7 communique about sharing the burden of the adjustment process."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, suspected BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reminded the market \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unhappy with export-damaging yen strength, which like the euro's rally to record highs versus the dollar on Monday resulted from bearish sentiment towards the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 By 1300 GMT, the euro was trading near the day's low around 132.44 yen , down nearly one percent on the day. It was also at $1.2483 , off about 0.7 percent, and down roughly four cents from Monday's record highs around $1.2900.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was 0.3 percent down on the day at 106.06 having hit a low around 105.70 before suspected BoJ action pulled it higher.  \par \par \uc2 There was no official confirmation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's data releases from the U.S. include business inventories at 1330 GMT, which are expected to show a median rise of 0.2 percent in November, and the University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey for January.  \par \par \uc2 The survey is seen reading 94.0 versus December's 92.6.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OFFENSIVE CONTINUES  \par \par \uc2 ECB chief economist Otmar Issing launched Friday's offensive, which boosted downward pressure on the euro, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not indifferent to the euro's strength.  \par \par \uc2 Issing reiterated comments from ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet saying that Trichet had shown "we are not indifferent about the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate but worried over the large volatility".  \par \par \uc2 However, Issing also told reporters the euro was not to blame for Germany's poor economic performance last year.  \par \par \uc2 He said a strong euro would offset some of the inflationary impact caused by higher taxes and administered prices that are expected to push up euro zone inflation in the first quarter of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Later on, ECB council member Guy Quaden said a further rise in the euro on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was not desirable. However, like Issing, he also moved to cushion the impact of his words, repeating the ECB's mantra of supporting a "strong and stable" euro.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts believe that euro zone officials have tried to issue verbal warnings to markets, while also giving soothing comments on the strong euro's effect on the economy, in order to protect business sentiment in the 15-nation bloc.  \par \par \uc2 G7 SUMMIT LOOMS  \par \par \uc2 The market's focus remains on how policymakers from the G7 industrial countries will react to the dollar's weakness at the meeting in Florida on February 6-7.  \par \par \uc2 G7 deputy finance ministers from each country are meeting in Brussels on January 26 to work on the agenda for the February meeting.  \par \par \uc2 But apparent nonchalance about the dollar's fall on the U.S. side has fanned expectations Washington is comfortable with a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 This view was highlighted by Japanese trade minister Shoichi Nakagawa who said he thought U.S. authorities did not really want a strong dollar despite Washington's official stance to the contrary.  \par \par \uc2 Japan is expected to be particularly vocal in complaining about the export-damaging yen strength and analysts see Japan's stance has become more hawkish following the recent increase in the government's war chest for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040116e01g002hw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
