\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar hits 3-yr low vs yen, BOJ seen taming yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2004\par 09:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 16 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a three-year low below 106.00 yen in early European trade on Friday before recovering some of its losses on suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the impetus for the drop came from the euro, which fell to a one-month low on the yen. The subsequent yen strength spilled over into dollar/yen.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was thought to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the dollar's low around 105.70, putting in orders that were 0.05 yen apart.  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  source said there was no change in Japan's policy to take measures against rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was struggling around 105.93 yen at 0825 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The euro hit its lowest level since the middle of December against the yen around 132.65 yen and was trading at 132.74.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hit a two-week low around $1.2520 , extending this week's 3-1/2 cent pullback from record highs as a result of complaints from European policymakers about the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.  \par \par \uc2 Market attention will focus on several speeches by European and U.S. policymakers' speeches later on Friday. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Economist Otmar Issing speaks at a ZEW institute forum in Germany at 0900 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 A news conference by French Finance Minister Francis Mer, Budget Minister Alain Lambert and Industry Minister Nicole Fontaine follows at 1030 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond President Alfred Broaddus speaks on the economy at 1300 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's data releases from the U.S. include business inventories at 1330 GMT, which are expected to show a median rise of 0.2 percent in November, and the University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey for January.  \par \par \uc2 The survey is seen reading 94.0 versus December's 92.6.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040116e01g001mv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
