\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro keeps lower after policymakers talk it down.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn  \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2004\par 09:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 15 (Reuters) - The euro traded close to the week's lows against the dollar on Thursday after complaints from euro zone officials about its strength took some air out of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two-month upward momentum.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone officials stepped up their rhetoric this week on the euro after it hit a record high around $1.29, making traders cautious about pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher still ahead of a meeting of economic policymakers from the Group of Seven (G7) industrial countries next month.  \par \par \uc2 Smaller-than-expected U.S. trade deficit figures on Wednesday also helped to support the dollar, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "The markets are reacting to the fact that European authorities have finally woken up and expressed concern about the pace at which the euro has been strengthening," said Adam Cole, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stragegist at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors meet in Florida on February 6-7 and the dollar's broad-based weakness is widely expected to be a major topic.  \par \par \uc2 At 0820 GMT, the euro was at $1.2640, two percent below the record high hit earlier in the week and unchanged from the U.S. close. The euro gained 13 percent against the dollar in the two months up until Monday, and has risen as much as 25 percent since the beginning of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Euro/yen stood at 134.23 yen, slightly down on the day and sharply down from last Friday's six-month peaks around 137.80.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady against the yen, hovering around 106.20 yen, amid ongoing wariness of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 TRICHET AND CO  \par \par \uc2 The upcoming G7 and the recent policymaker comments have put a brake on the euro in the past few days, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "Things have changed quite a bit this week. The spectre of the G7 coming out with something is keeping people on their toes," said Mitsuo Imaizumi, deputy general manager of international bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Daiwa Securities SMBC in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 For now, the market was waiting to see if more euro zone officials would join the jawboning foray.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet and Bank of Italy chief Antonio Fazio open a conference in Naples at 0900 GMT, which other central bankers will also attend.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also awaited a rash of U.S. data, including U.S. retail sales and consumer prices data for December, due out at 1330 GMT. Retail sales are forecast to rise by 0.9 percent on the month, and consumer prices by 0.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Weekly jobless claims are also due at 1330 GMT, and the Philadelphia Fed manufacturing survey for January at 1700 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Data from Germany, the euro zone's largest economy, did little to support the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Annual German GDP for 2003 was minus 0.1 percent, the first full year of contraction since 1993.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  NERVES  \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar dipped slightly under 106 yen on Wednesday, coming within sight of three-year lows around 105.90 set last week, traders said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was underpinned by wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to come under pressure from its peers over its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy when the G7 meets.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities conducted a record 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and are thought to have spent heavily since the start of this year.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki reiterated on Thursday that the dollar's fall was going too far given U.S. economic strength, and that Japan would take appropriate action, as needed, in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 This view was echoed by Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, who added that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets should be stable and reflect the relative strength of the U.S. economy.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040115e01f0020u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
