\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: Lid Is Off G7 On Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,170 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2004\par 19:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While it is almost a month before G7 central bankers and finance ministers meet in Boca Raton, Fla., the lid is off the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debate on the dollar as pre-meeting positioning by respective monetary authorities has hit full stride.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Not surprisingly, Europe set the charge that exploded the lid off G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bargaining. A two-year rise in euro/dollar of roughly 40% will tend to do this, even though the starting point was from a very depressed level (overvalued dollar). But wanting the dollar to stabilize or strengthen and doing something to make the dollar stabilize or strengthen is a wide gulf that the Feb. 6-7 meeting will not be able to bridge. It is far too early to look for an ECB rate cut to stem the euro rise (it probably will do little to turn the dollar). And \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is nothing the ECB warms to easily, and certainly nothing it easily embarks on when it is unilateral (without U.S. backing and participation).  \par \par \uc2 Indeed a close examination of European officials protesting the rise in the euro reveals a sense of resignation that a weaker dollar is natural in the face of a record U.S. current account deficit and no sign of a turn higher in public or private savings. Rather it is quite the opposite. The U.S. policy mix is aimed at running public and private consumption in overdrive. So there is an inevitability that the dollar must weaken. And this means mainly against the euro, as for all intents and purposes, Japan is pegging (crawling peg) the yen above 106.  \par \par \uc2 All Europe can hope to achieve at G7 is to get more backing for a slower decline in the dollar and to get Japan to stop pegging the yen and pick up its fair share of the (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -driven) adjustment process.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  surely has no intention of giving up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -based policy orientation with domestic demand soft and deflation hanging around like the plague. The sheer scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last 12 months (over $200 billion) is proof alone that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more than a whim of the government, but instead the driving force in monetary and fiscal policy. It has, after all, turned the Bank of Japan into an \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  arm of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with control over the money supply ceded to politicians. Yes it is possible that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  backs away temporarily from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of G7 so it (believes) can't be accused of manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  much as it did ahead of the September G7 meeting in Dubai. But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has evolved significantly from September that shedding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even for a G7 meeting seems unlikely. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  surely is counting on U.S. Treasury Secretary Snow looking the other way when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  simply because \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying U.S. Treasurys with its record \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and thereby funding a large portion of the U.S. budget deficit, and hence current account deficit. By the looks of it Snow's silence on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months is enough evidence for me to conclude that any beef the U.S. has on managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy rests with China not Japan. But China is buying Treasurys, too. Yes, but not to the extent that Japan is, and China is the more convenient political scapegoat for the Bush administration when it comes to explaining away the lack of new U.S. jobs created. Also they are communists for goodness sakes, and that is almost as bad as being liberal.  \par \par \uc2 And this leads us to the question of what is going on with U.S. dollar policy? A disorderly dollar decline is just what the doctor ordered, or so the Bush administration believes (hard not to agree). Any savvy politician knows that saying as much risks turning an orderly move lower in the dollar into a disorderly move. Enter the Fed. Am I the only one to notice that Fed officials have suddenly begun discussing the dollar in public (Greenspan, Bernanke and Guynn)? This does not just happen because Fed officials suddenly feel a need to address the question of the dollar in front of rabid dollar bears. It is the Fed doing the work of Treasury, very deliberately. That is right. The Fed has been given the okay from the White House to explain why the decline in the dollar to date is not a threat to U.S. inflation or the global recovery. Moreover, the Fed is eager to tell the markets that a weak dollar is benign for inflation because they are back to worrying about deflation in the wake of the December jobs report. In other words the Fed is out of ammo with real short-term rates negative (using core PCE price index). Additionally, from the Fed's perspective a weak dollar will reduce the need for Bush and the GOP from pursuing ever more tax cuts and spending hikes (though one would never know budgets are a priority with Bush given his ambitious plans for outer space). And while some might argue the dollar has fallen far enough, sending the Fed out to proselytize on the benign nature of a major dollar decline is in itself a signal that more is better. So, for the time being, if you want to know what the Treasury is thinking about the dollar listen to the Fed. Sorry but European officials will get nowhere with U.S. officials (Snow and Geeenspan) at G7 in Boca Raton.  \par \par \uc2 Undeniably there is enough uncertainty ahead of G7 over what will happen with the communique that the rise in euro/dollar will continue to be delayed. But once the meeting passes, and markets see G7 for what it is ... a dysfunctional family, the limits are off where euro/dollar goes and even the pace will difficult to contain. Europe may not like a weaker dollar or stronger euro, but unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  much less a rate cut are not in the cards ahead of and after G7. Japan is a harder call. But without the U.S. coming over the side of Europe on Japan sharing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -adjustment burden with the euro zone, it is hard to imagine \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  folding up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 /yen-peg tent. That said, the yen peg is an unsustainable policy and will be abandoned at some point creating a steep decline in dollar/yen, setting the stage for perhaps a more functioning G7 response at the May meeting in Washington.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.)  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040115e01f0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
