\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Ends Higher, Bolstered by Philadelphia Fed Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2004\par 23:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar's corrective recovery continued Thursday, with a batch of U.S. economic data helping propel the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to new highs for the week against its major European counterparts.  \par \par \uc2 After advancing only modestly following broadly supportive inflation, jobless and retail-sales data early in the U.S. session, the dollar got its real shot in the arm at noon from the Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia's business-conditions index for January, which came in at 38.8, above market estimates of 30.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's added impetus even lifted it against the yen, albeit with the suspected assistance of yet more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a big squeeze," said Paul Podolsky, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at FleetBoston Financial in Boston, noting that unwinding of short-dollar positions from short-term players drove the market. "It's nothing fundamental ... because there's nothing in the data that suggests the Fed is going to raise rates," the prerequisite for foreign investors to start investing en masse again in U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 Certainly, inflationary pressures appear weak, with consumer prices rising only 0.2% in December and the core figure up only 0.1%. Both these numbers were in line with economists' forecasts although retail sales last month rose by only 0.5% compared with calls for a jump of 0.8%. Jobless claims last week, however, fell by 11,000, more than the anticipated 3,000.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's correction was mirrored in other asset markets too as bond prices continued to rise, pushing yields on the 10-year Treasury note firmly below 4%. Gold, which forged 14-year highs last week, slumped over $10 to below $408.50 a troy ounce, a new month low.  \par \par \uc2 In late trading in New York Thursday, the euro was at $1.2570, down from $1.2650 late Wednesday, and down at 133.75 yen from 134.35 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to 106.40 yen from 106.25 yen, and gained more against the Swiss franc to hit 1.2460 francs, up from 1.2337 francs. The pound fell to $1.8230, bouncing back from an intraday low of $1.8180 but still weaker than $1.8320 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, which has been rebounding from all-time and multiyear lows for the past three sessions, now finds itself at the upper end of near-term ranges. If it is to extend its rally, the euro will have to fall below $1.2550, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Much of that may depend on the level of official rhetoric on the euro's overall strength between now and the meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers in Boca Raton, Fla., on Feb. 6-7.  \par \par \uc2 John Rothfield, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America, said that while profit-taking is to be expected due to the current risk/reward scenario ahead of the G-7 meeting, he believes the dollar will "continue down the path of least resistance" through the spring, with the euro climbing as high as $1.35.  \par \par \uc2 However, the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases "fairly significantly" as the euro moves above $1.30, either unilaterally by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or jointly with the U.S. Treasury.  \par \par \uc2 Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York, believes ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet's comment earlier this week on the euro's "brutal" moves already marks the beginning of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "It's preparing the market for a more pointed statement at the upcoming G-7 meeting," Mr. Woolfolk reckons.  \par \par \uc2 Despite Mr. Trichet appearing to take a harder line on the euro, the message from political, financial and corporate Europe is still mixed.  \par \par \uc2 Noel Forgeard, chief executive of European aircraft maker Airbus, told a press conference on Thursday: "We shouldn't react to the spot rate of the dollar. It's a volatile market. It can be at $1.40 dollar to the euro tomorrow but it won't be the end of the world. What's important is the average rate over 12 or 18 months."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Canadian dollar ended lower in generally quiet trading, extending its recent slide on selling related to Wednesday's weak merchandise-trade report and the global rebound for the U.S. dollar. The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.2951 late Thursday, up from C$1.2881 late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 One element in the weaker Canadian dollar story is that disappointing Canadian trade statistics have added to expectations of a Bank of Canada rate cut next Tuesday. A Dow Jones Newswires poll Thursday found nine out of twelve economists now predicting a one-quarter percentage point cut of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2.75% target overnight interest rate.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Tom Barkley and Paul Evans contributed to this article.)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040115e01f0000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
