\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan 15  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,089 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2004\par 10:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: South Korea took a surprise step to curb the won Thursday, slapping limits on offshore investors betting on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Seoul increased its arsenal for reining in the won ahead of next month's G7 meeting, where overt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar-buying could come under fire. The won dropped sharply on the announcement, which Seoul said was aimed at fighting rampant speculation. But as much of the recent won-buying has been portfolio-driven, analysts predict upward pressure on the won will resume. (=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA will run after 0900 GMT; see also "INTERVIEW:S Korea Aide/NDF -2: NDF Speculation Evident" that ran at 0616 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore hopes to attract S$8 billion in manufacturing investment commitments this year as the global economy strengthens, up from the S$7.51 billion it got last year amid the war in Iraq and the SARS outbreak. It also hopes to get another S$2 billion in total business spending in services, compared with the S$1.92 billion it achieved in 2003, the Economic Development Board said Thursday. ("=WRAP: Singapore Aims For Higher Invest As Econ Recovers" ran at 0700 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Glenn Hubbard, a former top U.S. economic adviser, said Thursday the dollar's recent slide has benefitted the U.S. economy. "An orderly decline has been good in the short term," he said, and "as long as it is smooth, it isn't a problem. ("=INTERVIEW: Ex-CEA Hubbard -2: BOJ Commitment Too Vague" ran at 0545 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's domestic corporate goods price index fell 0.1% in December from a year earlier, its smallest decline since August 2000 and a sign deflation pressures are easing, at least temporarily. ("=WRAP:December Japan CGPI Drop Smallest Since August 2000" ran at 0452 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: The Indian government took the politically bold decision ahead of elections to allow greater foreign investment in the country's banking and oil refining sectors. (WRAP will run around 1130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian's unemployment rate stayed at a 14-year low in December as a robust labor market added more jobs than expected, underscoring the strong domestic economy. The Australian Bureau of Statistics said Thursday that employment increased by 29,600 jobs in December from November, beating market consensus for a rise of 15,000. ("=DATA VIEW: Australian Employment Strong, Jobless Rate Low" ran at 0100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's jobless rate hit 3.6% last month, a level last seen in March 2003, as jobseekers surged during the winter vacation and ahead of college graduation in February, the National Statistical Office said Thursday. This compares to a jobless rate of 3.4% in November. ("S Korea/Dec Jobless -2: Highest Since March '03" ran at 2330 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Growth in China's money supply eased slightly by the end of December from the previous 11 months, official figures issued Thursday show, but maintained the fast expansion called for by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's looser monetary policy. ("China/Money Supply -2: End-Dec M2 Rises 19.6% To CNY22.1T" ran at 0603 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The Hong Kong government will miss its mid-March target for issuing HK$6 billion in securities backed by revenue from its toll bridges and tunnels after legislators requested more information before giving it their approval, Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury Federick Ma said. ("HK Govt Delays HK$6B Securitization; Lawmakers Seek Info" ran at 0224 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's recovering economy and an influx of mainland China visitors will underpin a solid rebound in the city's property market in 2004, international property consultants said this week. While prices in the luxury residential sector should rise in line with strong economic growth projections for Hong Kong, the market for office space in the city's central business district is expected to outperform, they said. ("=HK Office Sector To Lead 2004 Property Market Upturn" ran at 0626 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand business confidence deteriorated in the fourth quarter last year and is worse than usual for that time of year due to the high New Zealand dollar, according to a survey released Thursday. ("NZ's 4Q Confidence -2: Worse Than Usual For Time Of Year" ran at 2101)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar tiptoed lower against the yen in Asia Thursday, with speculative and real demand-backed players selling the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at highs but also taking care not to push it too low for fear of triggering more Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr/Yen Edges Dn; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF Fears Limit Slide" ran at 0554 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Samsung Electronics, the world's largest memory-chip maker, Thursday presented an upbeat outlook for first-quarter 2004 and unveiled an aggressive capital spending plan that far outstrips Intel Corp.'s. Earlier today, Samsung reported its fourth-quarter net profit rose 24% to 1.86 trillion won, helped by a robust performance from its flash memory chip and liquid crystal display businesses. ("Samsung Elec/Earnings -3: Strong Flash Memory, LCD Sales" ran at 0237 GMT; WRAP will run around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Aggressive price cutting, and new model launches helped China to post a passenger car sales growth rate of more than 75% last year, and many industry players expect another strong year. ("China-Made Car Sales Soar 75.3% In '03 To Record 1.97 Mln" ran at 0404 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese beer shipments in 2003 fell at the second-steepest annual pace in post-war history as a cool summer and a price hike in low-malt beer further weighed on an already weakening market. Shipment data from Japan's top brewers showed industry leader Asahi Breweries retained top market share for the third consecutive year with 39.9% market share, and Kirin Brewery in second place with 35.7%. ("=WRAP: Japan '03 Beer Shipments 2nd-Biggest Post-War Fall"  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian insurer and funds manager AMP Ltd. announced encouraging cash flows in its key financial services division on Thursday. But its mature and closed businesses in Australia and its New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  dragged on performance. ("=WRAP: Positive Signs In Australia's AMP Cash Flows" ran at 0426 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Worldwide sales of Japanese semiconductor manufacturing equipment are likely to surge 24% to Y1.062 trillion this fiscal year ending March, helped by Japanese chip makers' brisk capital expenditures, according to an industry association. Such a jump would mark a major rebound from last fiscal year's 2.9% drop, the Semiconductor Equipment Association of Japan said Thursday. ("Japan Chip Equip Sales Likely Up 24% This FY -Indus Group" ran at 0805 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Chartered Semiconductor Manufacturing, the world's third-biggest chip foundry, said International Business Machines will be a major part of its plans for its latest US$3 billion chip plant. IBM and Chartered Thursday announced a pact under which Chartered will make for IBM 90 nanometer Silicon-On-Insulator products, a technology used to enable high performance in devices including microprocessors and graphics processors. ("=INTERVIEW: Chartered Semi-2: Extends IBM Deal From 2002" ran at 0351 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sanyo Electric said Thursday it aims to boost its total global sales of mobile phones and digital cameras to Y800 billion in the next two years, or nearly a 50% increase from anticipated sales of such products for this fiscal year through March. ("Sanyo To Boost Cellphone,Digital Camera Sales 50% By FY05" ran at 0235 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai cement maker TPI Polene's offering of 300 million new shares was taken up completely on the first day of subscription, the offer's underwriter said Thursday. Retail and institutional investors were offered the shares at 37 baht each, raising THB11.1 billion for the company. The long-delayed share offering finally got off the ground after being postponed twice last year. (Thai TPI Polene Share Offer Fully Subscribed -Underwriter" ran at 0444 GMT) GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Airlines, the world's second-largest carrier by market capitalization, will issue passenger and cargo traffic data for December. (story will run around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Shares of New Zealand's Sky City Entertainment Group fell Thursday as news the company wants to acquire Australian-based MGM Grand Darwin casino made risk-averse investors nervous. ("=WRAP: NZ Sky's Plan To Buy Casino Causes Some Jitters" ran at 0317 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Shares in Australia's Southcorp clawed back some of their recent losses after the nation's biggest winemaker reassured investors that its earnings recovery plans were on track despite a surging Australian dollar. ("=WRAP: Australia's Southcorp Profit Recovery On Track" ran at 0527  \par \par \uc2 ISLAMABAD: When Pakistan's state-run Oil & Gas Development Co, or OGDC, makes its planned debut Monday on the Karachi Stock Exchange its hefty market capitalization and bright earnings outlook will make it a hard stock for investors to ignore. ("=Stardom Tipped For Pakistan OGDC's Bourse Debut" ran at 0533 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Flat panel maker HannStar Display said Thursday it sold 40 million Global Depositary Receipts for US$301.6 million to help purchase new equipment to build a new plant. ("Taiwan's HannStar Display Sells 40M GDRs For US$301.6M" ran at 0352 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shanghai Forte Land is touting itself to investors as a proxy for Shanghai's booming economy as it begins presentations for its Hong Kong share offering, but it is entirely vulnerable to any popping of the city's booming property market, according to research by its listing sponsors. (story will run around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Anhui BBCA Biochemical says Citigroup Global Markets Ltd. owns more than 20% of an outstanding issue of bonds convertible into its shares. ("China's Anhui BBCA Says Citigroup Holds Its Conv Bonds" ran at 0227 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Huaxia Bank, which listed in September, says its board has approved plans to issue 4.25 billion yuan in subordinated bonds. ("China Huaxia Bk/Bonds -2: Needs Hldr, Bank Regulator OK" ran at 0012 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's airline regulator is considering applications for a new generation of budget regional airlines, a General Administration of Civil Aviation of China, or CAAC, official said Thursday. ("China Regulator Considering Budget Airline Applications" ran at 0805 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: AsiaInfo Holdings has announced contracts with the China Iron and Steel Association and Ministry of Railways, which it hopes will be the start of many more to come for its recently acquired human resource management and business intelligence \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (INTERVIEW will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: The Singapore Exchange - one of three listed bourses in Asia Pacific - will release second-quarter/first half net profit later today. (results expected around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong shoemaker Yue Yuen scheduled to report full-year earnings at some point today; likely to show 32% jump in net profit to more than US$300 million thanks to full-year contribution from assets bought from its parent in 2002. (story will run when results are released)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: PetroChina, China's largest oil and gas company, said Thursday its total oil and gas output in 2003 rose 2.2% on year to reach 889.7 million barrels of oil equivalent. ("PetroChina 03 -2: Natural Gas Output 691.5 Bln Cubic Feet" first take ran at 0120 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China has halted imports of poultry products from Japan, Vietnam and South Korea after those countries reported outbreaks of bird flu, state media reported Thursday, citing the State Administration of Quality Supervision, Inspection and Quarantine. (China/Bird Flu -2: Ban Includes Japan, Vietnam, S Korea" ran at 0337 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: The world's third-biggest nickel producer Australia's WMC Resources has called on stainless steel producers, particularly in China, to start sharing the cost of soaring metal prices or face losing customers. ("=WRAP: Australia's WMC Sounds Nickel Price Warning" ran at 069 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Access for Australian sugar to the U.S. remains the most difficult issue in talks that could lead to a free trade agreement between the two countries, Trade Minister Mark Vaile said Thursday. ("Australian, US Trade Talks To Resume Monday; Sugar Sticky" ran at 0232 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040115e01f000d4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\