\par\par \b \uc2 Negative yen rates sink further on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  fallout.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima   \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2004\par 09:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 14 (Reuters) - Short-term yen interest rates plumbed record depths in negative territory on Wednesday as the fallout of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week widened the discount in the dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards market.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said, however, that the easier monetary conditions were unlikely to do much to boost to Japan's economy, although the skews in rates gave some financial powerhouses a chance to eke out profits in the arcane world of negative interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 Many banks were awash with yen funds on Wednesday as it was the settlement day for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions that took place on Friday, when Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to halt the dollar's slide to three-year lows against the yen.   \par \par \uc2 The discount rate for overnight dollar/yen forwards swelled to around 1.00 sen, or 1/100th of a yen, compared with recent typical levels of around 0.35.   \par \par \uc2 The forward market skew, through arbitrage with steadier U.S. money rates, fed into steeply negative yen rates in the eurodeposit market and eventually into the domestic Japanese market, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 As a result, the overnight call money rate fell to a record low of minus 0.30 percent, diving from the previous record of minus 0.08 percent set a day earlier.   \par \par \uc2 "The massive yen supply from Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is behind all this," said Izuru Kato, chief economist at Totan Research.   \par \par \uc2 "We are seeing negative rates but people who can actually trade at such rates are limited and these rates are not affecting the general lending rates or long-term rates. So, unfortunately, the macroeconomic impact is likely to be very small."   \par \par \uc2 Traders said dollar demand related to the settlement of U.S. bond coupon payments this week also appeared to have played a part in the forward market anomaly.   \par \par \uc2 NO SPOT \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  IMPACT   \par \par \uc2 They said the dollar demand was not of a permanent nature that would prompt traders to buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright on the spot market.   \par \par \uc2 "If the demand for dollars was so strong, you'd see a spike in forward rates in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the interbank desk at Trust & Custody Services Bank.   \par \par \uc2 "We aren't really seeing a permanent shortfall in dollars."   \par \par \uc2 Indeed, the spot dollar/yen exchange rate was steady on Wednesday, hovering at around 106.20 yen .   \par \par \uc2 Money market analysts calculate that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) sold more than one trillion yen ($9.41 billion), perhaps more than 1.5 trillion yen, in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday. Since the start of the year, almost five trillion yen or so may have been spent, they estimated.   \par \par \uc2 Since \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trades are settled two business days later and Monday was a Japanese national holiday, Wednesday saw a huge amount of yen fund flows into the private-sector banking system.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign banks, some of which have corporate policies limiting how much they can keep in reserves at the Bank of Japan due to growing doubts about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's creditworthiness in recent years, are typically forced to lend at negative rates to other global banks when they have a huge amount of surplus yen.   \par \par \uc2 As a result of the BOJ's unprecedented "quantitative easing" policy - in which it floods the market with liquidity in an attempt to spur lending and thus encourage economic growth - banks operating in Japan have some 30 trillion yen sitting in non-interest bearing current account deposits at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Those who borrowed at negative rates in the less restricted offshore market can turn around and supply funds to the domestic market, making some profit off the spreads even if they lend at slightly negative rates.   \par \par \uc2 "If y)rates, even if the call rates are negative," said a fixed income trader at a major Japanese brokerage house. ($1=106.22 Yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040114e01e001lf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
