\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar steadies as pre-G7 rhetoric simmers.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima   \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2004\par 07:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 14 (Reuters) - The dollar held steady on Wednesday as the market weighed comments by European officials concerned about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent drop to record lows against the euro and those by U.S. officials who appeared less concerned.   \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan led the U.S. camp, telling a Bundesbank seminar on Tuesday that the dollar's decline had yet to cause inflation or deter foreign capital inflow that is needed to fund the gaping U.S. current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke, German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder, German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement and French Finance Minister Francis Mer all expressed concerns about the economic impact of the euro's 40 percent rise versus the dollar over the past two years.   \par \par \uc2 The remarks come as the market ponders what the economic policymakers of the Group of Seven industrial countries might agree on at their meeting in Florida next month.   \par \par \uc2 "Essentially, Greenspan and the other Fed people are saying a weaker dollar is okay. From the euro zone, we're hearing groans, not just the government officials but from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  people as well now," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 "There seems to be a little tug of war going on in the run up to the February 6-7 G7 meeting," he said.   \par \par \uc2 At 0230 GMT, the euro stood at $1.2745/50 , down marginally from around $1.2765 late in New York on Tuesday and 1.5 cent off record highs near $1.29 set on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 G7 POLITICS   \par \par \uc2 The euro's relative stability this week - after pulling back from those historic heights - comes in the wake of remarks by ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet, who said on Monday that "brutal" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were unwelcome.   \par \par \uc2 Previously, ECB officials had been perceived in the market as taking a so-called benign neglect attitude towards euro strength.   \par \par \uc2 But analysts said the calm could be short-lived, given the dominant, broad-based decline in the dollar of late.   \par \par \uc2 "I think European officials are very concerned about the speed of the euro's rise, not the level. So they are unlikely to step into the market even if the euro rises above $1.30," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 "The euro may see more adjustment, but the overall uptrend will continue," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The yen was also little changed, hovering around 106.20 to the dollar , mirroring the euro/dollar market and also constrained by concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities.   \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has made little secret of its displeasure about the yen's recent rise against the dollar to its highest level in more than three years.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily last week to beat down the yen, adding to its 20 trillion yen ($188.3 billion) foray into the market last year.   \par \par \uc2 With a plan to raise the borrowing ceiling for the Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account not expected to be legislated in parliament before the end of the month, the ministry was expected to borrow short-term funds from the Bank of Japan.   \par \par \uc2 Under a deal struck last month, the ministry can sell up to 10 trillion yen worth of U.S. government bonds to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  under a repurchase agreement. The Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported on Wednesday that five trillion yen of that would change hands this week. ($1=106.22 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040114e01e000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
