\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Tanigaki: In Constant Contact With US, EU On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 278 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2004\par 09:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Wednesday that the government was in constant touch with the U.S. and Europe on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues.  \par \par \uc2 "We are in continuous contact with the U.S. and Europe", he told a regular press conference when asked if Tokyo would seek cooperation with Europe to stem the dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 "As for the U.S., it needs to deal with its twin deficits and we need to have sustainable, domestic demand-led growth," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said that if Washington dealt with its fiscal and current account deficits, it might remove a concern in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 Underscoring his point, International Monetary Fund economists in a report last week expressed alarm at growing U.S. budget deficits, saying they could hurt the global economy by roiling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and driving up interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Monday, the head of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jean-Claude Trichet, offered the ECB's strongest show of concern yet about the euro's recent record rise and was seconded by other European officials. Japan sought Tuesday to portray the ECB as being in line with Tokyo, which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively to support the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Despite reports that Tokyo had already sold U.S. Treasurys to raise cash for more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tanigaki said he couldn't comment on the issue.  \par \par \uc2 Kyodo News reported that the government Tuesday sold U.S. Treasurys worth about Y5 trillion to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to procure yen for dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040114e01e000bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
