\par\par \b \uc2 Japan MOF Mizoguchi: US Econ Relative Strength Unchanged  \b0\par\par\uc2 235 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2004\par 02:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A top Japanese finance official said Wednesday that the strength of the U.S.'s economy relative to Japan's is unchanged, underscoring Japan's view that the dollar should be stronger against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi also declined comment on reports that the government will sell U.S. Treasurys to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to raise funds for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported that the government will sell Wednesday a portion of its U.S. bond holdings to the BOJ to raise Y5 trillion for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kyodo News reported that the government has already sold holdings to raise the same amount.  \par \par \uc2 The government's fiscal 2003 supplemental budget proposal included a provision that would increase the ceiling for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds by Y21 trillion to Y100 trillion for this fiscal year, but the supplementary budget will not be approved until at least the end of the month.  \par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the government can only turn to the BOJ for additional funds. The two parties made an arrangement at the end of last year allowing the government to sell U.S. Treasurys to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise up to Y10 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Gibson and Alastair Gale  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040114e01e0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic